1
00:00:06,948 --> 00:00:09,551
Part anticipation,
part excitement, part worry.

2
00:00:10,652 --> 00:00:13,622
What kinds of things will happen there?

3
00:00:14,923 --> 00:00:16,291
Who will I meet?

4
00:00:17,459 --> 00:00:20,228
I'm excited, but I'm also really nervous.

5
00:00:22,464 --> 00:00:24,866
I'm curious because
I've never done anything like this.

6
00:00:26,401 --> 00:00:28,670
You know, someone you feel at ease
doing nothing with.

7
00:00:28,670 --> 00:00:30,605
That's the kind of relationship
I really want.

8
00:00:34,109 --> 00:00:35,443
I'm a little...

9
00:00:35,443 --> 00:00:37,679
I think I'm excited
and looking forward to it.

10
00:00:38,413 --> 00:00:40,715
Will I really meet someone
who's perfect for me?

11
00:00:40,715 --> 00:00:42,484
I do find myself wondering that.

12
00:00:47,088 --> 00:00:50,025
I think what you can learn
from a relationship...

13
00:00:51,326 --> 00:00:53,895
is that it's a process
of discovering sides of yourself...

14
00:00:53,895 --> 00:00:55,997
you never even knew existed.

15
00:00:56,197 --> 00:01:00,168
I thought this show would be
a wonderful opportunity.

16
00:01:04,105 --> 00:01:07,042
I'd love to fall in love
if I get the chance.

17
00:01:07,709 --> 00:01:10,979
I'm not afraid of whatever comes next.

18
00:01:11,413 --> 00:01:14,015
Before I knew it,

19
00:01:14,015 --> 00:01:16,151
I found myself going all in.

20
00:01:17,252 --> 00:01:20,055
If I'm going to date someone,
I want to do it right.

21
00:01:21,856 --> 00:01:23,091
I'm excited too.

22
00:01:23,091 --> 00:01:24,960
I wonder what it'll be like,

23
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:27,562
but honestly,
I'm more worried than excited.

24
00:01:27,562 --> 00:01:28,697
Can I really do this?

25
00:01:29,631 --> 00:01:31,566
But in the end,
I need to like the person...

26
00:01:31,566 --> 00:01:33,001
for me to be in a relationship.

27
00:01:33,301 --> 00:01:35,337
(Jung Yong Hwa)

28
00:01:35,337 --> 00:01:37,806
- He's tall and handsome.
- Seriously.

29
00:01:37,806 --> 00:01:40,408
The warm and handsome guy-next-door type.

30
00:01:40,408 --> 00:01:41,743
(DK)

31
00:01:41,743 --> 00:01:42,811
They're all so charming.

32
00:01:42,811 --> 00:01:44,045
Exactly. They all have something special.

33
00:01:44,045 --> 00:01:46,181
I was so excited to watch this...

34
00:01:46,181 --> 00:01:48,249
that I couldn't sleep.

35
00:01:48,249 --> 00:01:52,587
When I first heard the title
"Love Against Time,"

36
00:01:52,921 --> 00:01:54,923
I wondered if that meant
they got a set number of dates.

37
00:01:54,923 --> 00:01:56,992
You can only date
a certain number of times?

38
00:01:57,359 --> 00:02:00,128
How many people are they meeting?
What is this?

39
00:02:00,962 --> 00:02:02,163
I'm so excited.

40
00:02:02,163 --> 00:02:03,665
- We're finally watching it.
- I can't wait.

41
00:02:07,235 --> 00:02:08,803
Back then,

42
00:02:08,803 --> 00:02:12,507
I think I was around 22.

43
00:02:15,343 --> 00:02:17,312
(How would you like to be remembered?)

44
00:02:17,312 --> 00:02:21,549
(Leave behind a single photograph.)

45
00:02:22,651 --> 00:02:25,787
Because I was in the military,

46
00:02:25,787 --> 00:02:29,190
there were so many rules and restrictions.

47
00:02:33,094 --> 00:02:35,630
It was also right when COVID-19 broke out,

48
00:02:36,431 --> 00:02:40,068
and military hospitals had limited beds.

49
00:02:40,068 --> 00:02:42,837
If I couldn't get in,
I'd have to wait for another opening.

50
00:02:43,605 --> 00:02:47,976
The whole process took quite a while.

51
00:02:47,976 --> 00:02:49,811
I think that's why
it had progressed so far.

52
00:02:51,313 --> 00:02:54,916
That day, the doctor started typing
furiously in my chart.

53
00:02:55,250 --> 00:02:58,320
I wondered what was wrong and asked,
"Is something off?"

54
00:02:58,320 --> 00:02:59,354
They told me to be quiet for a moment.

55
00:03:05,093 --> 00:03:07,262
Only after a long silence did they say,

56
00:03:12,133 --> 00:03:13,268
"You have cancer."

57
00:03:14,703 --> 00:03:16,338
Honestly, I couldn't quite...

58
00:03:17,038 --> 00:03:19,441
Cancer? It didn't feel real.

59
00:03:19,441 --> 00:03:22,277
Was it the same cancer
you see in movies and TV shows?

60
00:03:31,152 --> 00:03:32,153
You'd never know.

61
00:03:32,153 --> 00:03:33,088
I honestly had no idea.

62
00:03:33,088 --> 00:03:35,090
They just seem completely normal.

63
00:03:36,925 --> 00:03:38,059
Exactly.

64
00:03:39,494 --> 00:03:41,563
It really does come out of nowhere.

65
00:03:41,830 --> 00:03:43,264
- Exactly.
- My goodness.

66
00:03:49,237 --> 00:03:52,540
I sat there stunned,
then asked, "Could I die?"

67
00:03:52,540 --> 00:03:53,975
"You could."

68
00:03:54,643 --> 00:03:57,178
(Without warning one day,)

69
00:03:57,178 --> 00:04:01,449
(death came knocking.)

70
00:04:04,786 --> 00:04:06,421
It has a very high mortality rate.

71
00:04:06,421 --> 00:04:09,457
Apparently,
they told my mom something like,

72
00:04:09,457 --> 00:04:12,327
"You may need to prepare yourself."

73
00:04:12,327 --> 00:04:14,696
I think it was around stage four.

74
00:04:14,696 --> 00:04:18,266
Since the survival rate
is below 50 percent,

75
00:04:18,266 --> 00:04:19,668
we should prepare for the worst
and try chemotherapy.

76
00:04:19,668 --> 00:04:21,269
"The survival rate is 30 percent?"

77
00:04:21,269 --> 00:04:23,104
"Then what am I supposed to do?"

78
00:04:23,104 --> 00:04:25,140
"Is there any point
in getting treatment now?"

79
00:04:25,140 --> 00:04:29,844
I had no idea what to do next,
so for two or three days,

80
00:04:29,844 --> 00:04:33,248
I just sat dazed,
even when I went to work.

81
00:04:33,248 --> 00:04:35,817
What am I supposed to do? And then...

82
00:04:38,620 --> 00:04:41,256
(The time we had...)

83
00:04:41,256 --> 00:04:44,526
(began to move at a different pace.)

84
00:04:47,862 --> 00:04:49,698
(And just like that,)

85
00:04:49,698 --> 00:04:53,101
(our love lives came to a halt.)

86
00:04:54,002 --> 00:04:56,004
But I suppose...

87
00:04:56,004 --> 00:04:57,939
dating became completely different
after I got sick.

88
00:04:57,939 --> 00:05:00,008
From their perspective, it might be...

89
00:05:00,008 --> 00:05:01,343
a dealbreaker.

90
00:05:01,343 --> 00:05:04,879
Someone I was briefly talking to said
genetics really mattered.

91
00:05:04,879 --> 00:05:06,881
When you have children,

92
00:05:06,881 --> 00:05:10,118
You never know what might happen,"
they said.

93
00:05:10,118 --> 00:05:11,786
I was wearing a wig.

94
00:05:11,786 --> 00:05:13,622
But I didn't want them to find out.

95
00:05:13,622 --> 00:05:16,257
What feels most unfair
is getting sick so young.

96
00:05:16,257 --> 00:05:18,560
It felt like my twenties
had been stolen from me.

97
00:05:19,828 --> 00:05:22,464
I remember wondering,
"How could the world"...

98
00:05:22,464 --> 00:05:24,232
"do this to me?"

99
00:05:24,232 --> 00:05:26,201
"If there is a God, why me?"

100
00:05:28,236 --> 00:05:33,241
(With our time being finite,)

101
00:05:34,609 --> 00:05:39,414
(what kind of love will we dream of?)

102
00:05:41,816 --> 00:05:45,654
Going through a serious illness...

103
00:05:45,654 --> 00:05:47,922
changed the way I saw so many things.

104
00:05:50,058 --> 00:05:52,193
Nothing in the world scares me anymore.

105
00:05:52,427 --> 00:05:57,032
In a way,
I was already living on borrowed time.

106
00:05:57,032 --> 00:05:59,200
"These are my last years in my twenties."

107
00:05:59,200 --> 00:06:01,503
It would be such a waste,"
I keep thinking.

108
00:06:01,503 --> 00:06:06,274
I'd be grateful to meet someone
who loves all of me.

109
00:06:06,274 --> 00:06:11,413
So my desire to build
a happy family has grown.

110
00:06:11,413 --> 00:06:12,881
That's what I want.

111
00:06:13,548 --> 00:06:17,118
Just an ordinary home, an ordinary family,

112
00:06:17,118 --> 00:06:19,688
and an ordinary child.

113
00:06:20,588 --> 00:06:21,756
Just an ordinary life.

114
00:06:24,793 --> 00:06:26,061
What did you think?

115
00:06:27,395 --> 00:06:32,534
College is when you should be having
the time of your life,

116
00:06:33,501 --> 00:06:35,236
but instead,
they were fighting for theirs.

117
00:06:35,804 --> 00:06:39,541
The moment they turned the page,
I thought,

118
00:06:39,541 --> 00:06:41,443
"So that's what this show is.
It's different."

119
00:06:41,443 --> 00:06:42,978
This is...

120
00:06:42,978 --> 00:06:44,312
I knew I had to get ready to cry.

121
00:06:44,312 --> 00:06:45,647
This one's going to make us bawl.

122
00:06:46,281 --> 00:06:50,619
Love Against Time feels like
such a sincere show.

123
00:06:51,686 --> 00:06:53,021
Once I heard what it was about,

124
00:06:53,021 --> 00:06:56,157
I became so curious
about their love stories.

125
00:06:56,691 --> 00:06:58,827
You know what I mean.

126
00:06:58,827 --> 00:07:03,064
Your twenties
and early thirties are when...

127
00:07:03,064 --> 00:07:04,933
hanging out with friends is so much fun,

128
00:07:04,933 --> 00:07:08,103
and every day is a dopamine rush.

129
00:07:08,103 --> 00:07:10,805
But dating during those years
can feel like...

130
00:07:10,805 --> 00:07:12,741
you're handing your sadness and pain
to someone else,

131
00:07:12,741 --> 00:07:15,076
so you end up avoiding relationships.

132
00:07:15,377 --> 00:07:19,714
All these people have come together
to find love,

133
00:07:19,714 --> 00:07:21,716
and it makes every one
of their love stories...

134
00:07:21,716 --> 00:07:23,084
feel that much more precious.

135
00:07:23,084 --> 00:07:24,152
Exactly.

136
00:07:24,152 --> 00:07:27,589
Ye Na, I heard you were sick
when you were young too.

137
00:07:27,589 --> 00:07:28,456
That's right.

138
00:07:28,456 --> 00:07:30,759
When I was little,

139
00:07:30,759 --> 00:07:35,196
- I had childhood lymphoma.
- Really?

140
00:07:35,196 --> 00:07:38,199
So I can relate to them,

141
00:07:38,199 --> 00:07:40,869
and it really hits close to home.

142
00:07:43,138 --> 00:07:47,375
These participants
really could have lost their lives.

143
00:07:47,375 --> 00:07:50,779
They've made it through times
when tomorrow wasn't guaranteed.

144
00:07:51,546 --> 00:07:53,348
- I really hope this works out for them.
- Me too.

145
00:07:53,348 --> 00:07:55,317
I hope they all find happiness in love.

146
00:07:55,317 --> 00:07:56,885
- Please.
- Please, let them be happy.

147
00:07:56,885 --> 00:07:58,687
I hope all four of them become couples.

148
00:07:58,687 --> 00:08:00,522
I don't think anything
could scare them anymore.

149
00:08:01,089 --> 00:08:04,359
(Love Against Time begins now.)

150
00:08:08,930 --> 00:08:09,864
(Kim Seon Ho)

151
00:08:09,864 --> 00:08:12,334
Hello, I'm Kim Seon Ho.

152
00:08:12,901 --> 00:08:14,336
I'm pretty shy.

153
00:08:14,336 --> 00:08:15,704
I get embarrassed easily,

154
00:08:15,704 --> 00:08:18,273
and I worry about...

155
00:08:18,273 --> 00:08:19,874
what the other person might think of me.

156
00:08:19,874 --> 00:08:21,610
I wonder how things will go,

157
00:08:30,952 --> 00:08:35,857
but if someone is going through
the same thing as me,

158
00:08:35,857 --> 00:08:38,560
I wouldn't see that as a negative.

159
00:08:42,998 --> 00:08:45,166
I've been there myself.

160
00:08:46,434 --> 00:08:48,236
If we click, it doesn't matter.

161
00:08:48,236 --> 00:08:49,404
If anything,

162
00:08:49,404 --> 00:08:51,006
I'd date without holding back.

163
00:08:52,440 --> 00:08:56,211
(Where Love Against Time will begin)

164
00:08:56,211 --> 00:09:00,081
(The house in Yongsan-gu, Seoul)

165
00:09:01,416 --> 00:09:08,390
(What moments will unfold here?)

166
00:09:15,730 --> 00:09:18,633
(The first housemate is Seon Ho.)

167
00:09:23,204 --> 00:09:24,706
(Hesitates)

168
00:09:32,447 --> 00:09:35,917
(He chooses the innermost seat.)

169
00:09:40,855 --> 00:09:43,959
Hello, I'm Jung Ye Ji.

170
00:09:44,192 --> 00:09:46,594
None of my friends know about it at all.

171
00:09:46,594 --> 00:09:49,531
After I finished treatment
and returned to normal life,

172
00:09:49,531 --> 00:09:52,801
I stopped talking about it much.

173
00:09:53,034 --> 00:09:56,371
They'll be truly shocked
when they see me on the show.

174
00:09:58,607 --> 00:10:01,109
They're all people
who've been through the same pain.

175
00:10:02,644 --> 00:10:04,946
We could ask each other things like,
"What treatment did you get?"

176
00:10:04,946 --> 00:10:07,182
I think we'll have so much to talk about.

177
00:10:08,183 --> 00:10:12,787
I feel like this will be
a healing process for me too,

178
00:10:14,189 --> 00:10:17,225
so I'm really looking forward to it.

179
00:10:23,598 --> 00:10:27,602
(The second housemate is Ye Ji.)

180
00:10:35,176 --> 00:10:36,544
(Startled)

181
00:10:36,544 --> 00:10:37,545
Hello.

182
00:10:39,281 --> 00:10:40,248
Hello.

183
00:10:52,027 --> 00:10:53,795
When did you get here?

184
00:10:54,629 --> 00:10:56,464
I'm not sure.
I can't see the clock from here.

185
00:10:58,933 --> 00:11:00,068
I'm...

186
00:11:00,068 --> 00:11:01,136
the second person here.

187
00:11:05,340 --> 00:11:07,342
Have you been sitting here
this whole time?

188
00:11:11,947 --> 00:11:14,182
(Thump, thump)

189
00:11:15,050 --> 00:11:16,117
For quite a while.

190
00:11:19,621 --> 00:11:21,189
- He's cute.
- So cute.

191
00:11:21,189 --> 00:11:22,557
The way he's clutching his heart.

192
00:11:22,557 --> 00:11:24,059
Why does he keep clutching his chest?

193
00:11:24,859 --> 00:11:26,628
- He must be so nervous.
- I'd be so nervous too.

194
00:11:26,628 --> 00:11:28,163
He must be incredibly nervous.

195
00:11:28,163 --> 00:11:29,130
That's adorable.

196
00:11:29,731 --> 00:11:30,999
He can't hide his smile.

197
00:11:30,999 --> 00:11:33,034
- How cute.
- Isn't he adorable?

198
00:11:35,236 --> 00:11:36,438
Aren't you nervous?

199
00:11:38,039 --> 00:11:39,608
I feel like my heart's going to explode.

200
00:11:42,110 --> 00:11:43,111
(Sneaks a glance)

201
00:11:45,146 --> 00:11:46,181
My first impression was...

202
00:11:47,215 --> 00:11:48,483
that she seemed...

203
00:11:48,483 --> 00:11:51,486
petite, graceful,

204
00:11:51,486 --> 00:11:53,054
and kind of cute.

205
00:11:53,054 --> 00:11:55,357
She seemed very poised and proper.

206
00:11:59,461 --> 00:12:05,000
I couldn't tell anything at all
when I first saw him.

207
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,369
He looked perfectly healthy,

208
00:12:07,369 --> 00:12:12,007
so I wondered what kind of pain
he'd been through.

209
00:12:16,745 --> 00:12:19,781
This house is really pretty.

210
00:12:19,781 --> 00:12:21,016
The house, I mean.

211
00:12:26,855 --> 00:12:28,423
(Nervous)

212
00:12:30,258 --> 00:12:31,726
(Hesitates)

213
00:12:34,062 --> 00:12:35,697
What's your name?

214
00:12:36,798 --> 00:12:38,266
I'm Jung Ye Ji.

215
00:12:38,266 --> 00:12:39,734
Jung Ye Ji?

216
00:12:39,734 --> 00:12:41,236
And what's your name?

217
00:12:41,236 --> 00:12:43,104
I'm Kim Seon Ho.

218
00:12:43,104 --> 00:12:44,539
- Seon Ho.
- Yes.

219
00:12:44,539 --> 00:12:45,707
Kim Seon Ho.

220
00:12:46,641 --> 00:12:48,743
Jung Ye Ji. Got it.

221
00:12:50,712 --> 00:12:53,882
(They finally managed
to introduce themselves.)

222
00:12:56,518 --> 00:12:59,387
The garden is really beautiful.

223
00:12:59,387 --> 00:13:00,855
I can't see it from here, though.

224
00:13:00,855 --> 00:13:02,424
Really? You can't see it at all?

225
00:13:02,424 --> 00:13:05,093
I can see some of the trees.

226
00:13:06,594 --> 00:13:07,963
I'll take a look later.

227
00:13:07,963 --> 00:13:09,297
Yes, it's really pretty.

228
00:13:18,640 --> 00:13:23,078
(They keep running out of things to say.)

229
00:13:26,247 --> 00:13:27,682
(Sneaks a glance)

230
00:13:44,966 --> 00:13:46,434
(Snickers)

231
00:13:48,036 --> 00:13:50,939
Imagine how awkward
that silence must've been.

232
00:13:51,172 --> 00:13:52,307
They're so cute.

233
00:13:52,307 --> 00:13:53,742
Why am I getting shy too?

234
00:13:53,742 --> 00:13:54,809
Exactly.

235
00:13:54,809 --> 00:13:56,444
Ye Na, why don't you go join them?

236
00:13:57,178 --> 00:13:59,514
They look so good together.

237
00:14:02,450 --> 00:14:06,755
(A pleasant silence settles between them.)

238
00:14:11,226 --> 00:14:12,093
(Han Do Gon)

239
00:14:12,093 --> 00:14:13,895
Hello, I'm Han Do Gon.

240
00:14:15,196 --> 00:14:17,232
When I find something I like,

241
00:14:17,232 --> 00:14:19,467
I'm very driven,

242
00:14:19,467 --> 00:14:21,469
so I take action right away.

243
00:14:21,469 --> 00:14:25,407
I figured that approach
might help me meet someone great.

244
00:14:29,878 --> 00:14:31,513
If anything,

245
00:14:31,513 --> 00:14:33,148
because we know the situation better,

246
00:14:33,148 --> 00:14:35,083
we know what to be careful about...

247
00:14:35,083 --> 00:14:37,385
and where to be more considerate.

248
00:14:38,019 --> 00:14:41,356
That could lead
to an even deeper relationship.

249
00:14:46,361 --> 00:14:49,130
(The third housemate is Do Gon.)

250
00:14:52,600 --> 00:14:54,002
Goodness.

251
00:14:54,936 --> 00:14:56,705
(Nervous)

252
00:14:58,540 --> 00:14:59,608
This is driving me crazy.

253
00:15:02,243 --> 00:15:03,445
Hello.

254
00:15:03,445 --> 00:15:04,245
(Bows)

255
00:15:06,181 --> 00:15:07,148
Hello.

256
00:15:09,818 --> 00:15:11,152
You got here before me.

257
00:15:14,456 --> 00:15:15,957
This is so awkward.

258
00:15:16,257 --> 00:15:17,525
I'll sit in the middle.

259
00:15:19,761 --> 00:15:20,829
Nice to meet you.

260
00:15:20,829 --> 00:15:21,730
Nice to meet you.

261
00:15:21,730 --> 00:15:22,931
What's your name?

262
00:15:22,931 --> 00:15:24,532
I'm Han Do Gon.

263
00:15:24,532 --> 00:15:28,003
- Do Gon? Han Do Gon?
- Yes. Do Gon.

264
00:15:28,003 --> 00:15:31,239
I'm Kim Seon Ho.

265
00:15:31,239 --> 00:15:33,108
Kim Seon Ho. Nice to meet you.

266
00:15:33,108 --> 00:15:34,809
I'm Jung Ye Ji.

267
00:15:34,809 --> 00:15:36,578
Ye Ji. Jung Ye Ji.

268
00:15:39,648 --> 00:15:40,649
Would you like some water?

269
00:15:40,649 --> 00:15:41,583
Yes, please.

270
00:15:44,085 --> 00:15:48,890
(Do Gon gets water for Ye Ji first.)

271
00:15:49,858 --> 00:15:50,792
Thank you.

272
00:16:01,102 --> 00:16:02,437
There's something about Seon Ho's eyes.

273
00:16:02,437 --> 00:16:03,605
His expression is almost...

274
00:16:03,605 --> 00:16:05,540
- Wary?
- Like he's on guard.

275
00:16:05,540 --> 00:16:07,909
Seon Ho, you'd better step it up.

276
00:16:07,909 --> 00:16:09,911
Come on, Seon Ho.

277
00:16:09,911 --> 00:16:11,379
Stop clutching your heart and make a move.

278
00:16:11,379 --> 00:16:12,881
A stranger just arrived,
so he's a little...

279
00:16:12,881 --> 00:16:15,517
He'd just gotten comfortable with
the two of them, but someone else is here.

280
00:16:15,517 --> 00:16:17,252
- Is he uncomfortable again?
- I think so.

281
00:16:17,252 --> 00:16:19,220
Then every time
he starts feeling comfortable...

282
00:16:21,856 --> 00:16:24,359
(Endless heart clutching is expected.)

283
00:16:29,631 --> 00:16:30,665
Thank you.

284
00:16:31,433 --> 00:16:32,400
That's better.

285
00:16:44,079 --> 00:16:46,014
Hello, I'm Seo Ro Bin.

286
00:16:46,915 --> 00:16:48,783
Actually,
I've never been in a relationship.

287
00:16:48,783 --> 00:16:50,318
I've been single my whole life,

288
00:16:50,318 --> 00:16:51,920
so I've never even been on a date.

289
00:16:51,920 --> 00:16:53,722
This is a completely
new experience for me.

290
00:16:55,657 --> 00:16:58,026
I think it'll be easier
to share my story...

291
00:16:58,026 --> 00:17:00,862
with people who've also
been through an illness,

292
00:17:00,862 --> 00:17:02,397
because we'll have something in common.

293
00:17:08,703 --> 00:17:09,971
I'm so curious.

294
00:17:10,639 --> 00:17:13,575
Will I get to experience love and romance?

295
00:17:18,346 --> 00:17:21,483
(The fourth housemate is Ro Bin.)

296
00:17:21,483 --> 00:17:22,517
(Bang)

297
00:17:25,754 --> 00:17:27,055
Did you hear the door?

298
00:17:28,256 --> 00:17:29,224
That sounds like heels.

299
00:17:36,164 --> 00:17:39,734
- Hello.
- Hello.

300
00:17:40,835 --> 00:17:41,937
- Nice to meet you.
- Gosh.

301
00:17:44,172 --> 00:17:45,607
Sit wherever you're comfortable.

302
00:17:46,241 --> 00:17:47,909
Then I'll take the seat at the end.

303
00:17:58,920 --> 00:18:00,221
The most memorable moment?

304
00:18:01,256 --> 00:18:02,958
When Ro Bin walked in.

305
00:18:04,426 --> 00:18:06,361
She seemed so bright and refreshing.

306
00:18:07,395 --> 00:18:10,732
She reminded me of a sports drink.

307
00:18:18,707 --> 00:18:19,741
(Startled)

308
00:18:20,775 --> 00:18:21,810
What's happening?

309
00:18:21,810 --> 00:18:23,111
His whole gaze changed.

310
00:18:23,111 --> 00:18:24,512
That came straight from the heart.

311
00:18:24,512 --> 00:18:25,647
He said it before he even realized it.

312
00:18:26,848 --> 00:18:28,683
Doesn't it seem like
they're all into different people?

313
00:18:29,618 --> 00:18:30,719
My goodness.

314
00:18:30,719 --> 00:18:34,289
Seon Ho likes Ye Ji, Ye Ji likes Do Gon,
and Do Gon likes...

315
00:18:34,289 --> 00:18:35,657
Ro Bin.

316
00:18:35,991 --> 00:18:37,892
Even if we're wrong,
this is already so much fun.

317
00:18:44,399 --> 00:18:45,900
(Stares)

318
00:18:47,102 --> 00:18:48,536
What's your name?

319
00:18:48,536 --> 00:18:50,605
I'm Seo Ro Bin.

320
00:18:50,605 --> 00:18:51,873
R-O.

321
00:18:51,873 --> 00:18:54,242
Ro Bin.

322
00:18:54,242 --> 00:18:55,510
Seo Ro Bin?

323
00:19:00,215 --> 00:19:02,450
There seem to be
a lot more cushions beside me.

324
00:19:02,450 --> 00:19:03,652
You can have this one.

325
00:19:03,652 --> 00:19:05,086
Thank you.

326
00:19:07,989 --> 00:19:09,157
Thank you.

327
00:19:10,759 --> 00:19:16,898
Later, should we play a game to decide...

328
00:19:16,898 --> 00:19:19,200
who cooks and who shares each room?

329
00:19:19,200 --> 00:19:20,035
A game?

330
00:19:20,035 --> 00:19:22,170
I'm terrible at games.

331
00:19:22,170 --> 00:19:23,338
Me too.

332
00:19:23,338 --> 00:19:25,240
Or what do you call that one?

333
00:19:25,240 --> 00:19:26,608
"Up or down?"

334
00:19:26,608 --> 00:19:27,776
"Flip your hands up or down?"

335
00:19:29,177 --> 00:19:31,212
Isn't it "jen-di?"

336
00:19:31,212 --> 00:19:32,681
What's "jen-di?"
I've never heard that before.

337
00:19:32,681 --> 00:19:33,982
Really?

338
00:19:33,982 --> 00:19:34,916
Jen-di?

339
00:19:34,916 --> 00:19:36,618
- Jen-di?
- Jen-di.

340
00:19:36,618 --> 00:19:38,620
Jen-di?

341
00:19:38,620 --> 00:19:39,587
You all call it "up or down?"

342
00:19:39,587 --> 00:19:41,356
We called it "den-di."

343
00:19:41,356 --> 00:19:43,325
- What? "Den-di?"
- "Den-di?"

344
00:19:44,092 --> 00:19:45,593
That's even more unfamiliar.

345
00:19:46,795 --> 00:19:48,363
- What is it?
- Den-di?

346
00:19:49,264 --> 00:19:51,933
For me, it's "Pick a side, pick a side,
al-ko-reu-se-yo."

347
00:19:53,201 --> 00:19:54,603
Pick a side," and then what?

348
00:19:54,603 --> 00:19:56,905
"Pick a side, pick a side,
al-ko-reu-se-yo."

349
00:19:56,905 --> 00:19:58,206
Al-ko-reu-se-yo?

350
00:19:59,374 --> 00:20:00,408
What's that?

351
00:20:00,408 --> 00:20:02,544
That's hilarious.
He said "al-ko-reu-se-yo."

352
00:20:02,978 --> 00:20:05,647
You go, "Al-ko-reu-se-yo," like this.

353
00:20:05,647 --> 00:20:08,450
So all four of us say it differently.

354
00:20:08,450 --> 00:20:09,784
- We do.
- We should ask everyone else too.

355
00:20:09,784 --> 00:20:12,454
This is so much fun.

356
00:20:12,454 --> 00:20:13,588
I've never heard any of those.

357
00:20:13,588 --> 00:20:17,025
We're all from completely different
parts of the country, then.

358
00:20:17,025 --> 00:20:17,726
I think so.

359
00:20:17,726 --> 00:20:19,027
That's fascinating.

360
00:20:22,230 --> 00:20:24,099
(Kim Ryun Gi)

361
00:20:24,099 --> 00:20:25,800
Hello, I'm Kim Ryun Gi.

362
00:20:27,369 --> 00:20:29,204
My biggest strength in dating?

363
00:20:29,871 --> 00:20:30,972
The gift of gab?

364
00:20:32,173 --> 00:20:34,009
I'm good at getting my point across.

365
00:20:34,576 --> 00:20:37,145
That just occurred to me.

366
00:20:37,145 --> 00:20:38,580
Did that sound a little arrogant?

367
00:20:40,882 --> 00:20:44,052
I want to date someone...

368
00:20:44,052 --> 00:20:48,356
I can show my completely
unfiltered self to.

369
00:20:49,524 --> 00:20:53,028
Right now,
with all the uncertainty I feel,

370
00:20:53,028 --> 00:20:55,764
this is something new
I'm challenging myself to try.

371
00:20:58,266 --> 00:21:02,604
(The fifth housemate is Ryun Gi.)

372
00:21:06,141 --> 00:21:07,342
- You heard that, right?
- Yes.

373
00:21:08,076 --> 00:21:10,045
Someone's coming.

374
00:21:10,045 --> 00:21:11,546
(Laughter)

375
00:21:15,083 --> 00:21:16,184
Hello.

376
00:21:16,918 --> 00:21:18,353
Hello.

377
00:21:18,954 --> 00:21:20,922
- Nice to meet you.
- Hello.

378
00:21:20,922 --> 00:21:22,023
Hello.

379
00:21:24,225 --> 00:21:25,360
It's a man.

380
00:21:25,360 --> 00:21:26,761
Hello.

381
00:21:26,761 --> 00:21:28,096
Sit wherever you're comfortable.

382
00:21:29,264 --> 00:21:31,833
Just based on first impressions,

383
00:21:31,833 --> 00:21:36,471
Ryun Gi was the closest
to my type physically.

384
00:21:36,471 --> 00:21:39,174
He looked so kind and gentle.

385
00:21:40,442 --> 00:21:42,811
Have you been here long?

386
00:21:42,811 --> 00:21:44,245
He's been here the longest.

387
00:21:47,248 --> 00:21:48,783
What's your name?

388
00:21:48,783 --> 00:21:52,554
I'm Kim Ryun Gi.

389
00:21:52,554 --> 00:21:54,656
Ryun Gi, starting with an R.

390
00:21:54,656 --> 00:21:56,625
(Ro Bin and Ryun Gi, both with R's)

391
00:21:56,625 --> 00:21:58,460
Everyone's names are so unusual.

392
00:21:58,460 --> 00:21:59,594
Really?

393
00:21:59,861 --> 00:22:01,630
You know...

394
00:22:01,630 --> 00:22:02,631
the Olympic...

395
00:22:03,565 --> 00:22:04,299
Wait.

396
00:22:04,299 --> 00:22:05,700
O-ryun-gi, like the Olympic rings?

397
00:22:08,370 --> 00:22:09,804
- I don't think I'll forget it.
- Right?

398
00:22:10,405 --> 00:22:12,807
(A memorable introduction)

399
00:22:14,843 --> 00:22:17,178
Hello, I'm Kim Jong Eun.

400
00:22:17,178 --> 00:22:19,414
I'm always full of confidence.

401
00:22:19,781 --> 00:22:24,052
It's better than having no confidence,

402
00:22:24,052 --> 00:22:25,720
so I always keep mine topped up.

403
00:22:25,987 --> 00:22:27,656
(Bursts out laughing)

404
00:22:27,656 --> 00:22:28,723
Out of nowhere?

405
00:22:28,723 --> 00:22:30,158
That sudden confidence?

406
00:22:30,425 --> 00:22:32,227
Too much confidence isn't good either.

407
00:22:32,661 --> 00:22:34,329
Even his walk is distinctive.

408
00:22:39,601 --> 00:22:42,203
I almost died,
and now I've come back to life.

409
00:22:42,203 --> 00:22:45,373
I live by the motto, "What can't I do?"

410
00:22:47,275 --> 00:22:51,680
(The sixth housemate is Jong Eun.)

411
00:22:52,180 --> 00:22:53,481
(Startled)

412
00:22:54,549 --> 00:22:55,917
Someone's here.

413
00:22:55,917 --> 00:22:57,319
They open the door with so much force.

414
00:22:58,653 --> 00:23:00,255
Hello.

415
00:23:04,225 --> 00:23:06,494
Hello.

416
00:23:07,629 --> 00:23:09,731
(Quickly checks her appearance)

417
00:23:11,333 --> 00:23:13,902
(He casually takes the center seat.)

418
00:23:13,902 --> 00:23:14,970
There are slippers over there.

419
00:23:14,970 --> 00:23:16,004
Really?

420
00:23:16,771 --> 00:23:17,839
I'll go put them on.

421
00:23:22,410 --> 00:23:24,879
(His powerful entrance...)

422
00:23:24,879 --> 00:23:26,681
(and contrasting retreat)

423
00:23:29,484 --> 00:23:30,585
No.

424
00:23:32,253 --> 00:23:34,155
(Awkwardly slips them on)

425
00:23:37,692 --> 00:23:41,096
(Returns as if nothing happened)

426
00:23:44,933 --> 00:23:46,568
(Back to the center seat)

427
00:23:46,568 --> 00:23:47,736
You can have some water.

428
00:23:49,137 --> 00:23:51,206
Have a sip first.

429
00:23:54,976 --> 00:23:58,146
(A striking presence
from the moment he arrived)

430
00:23:58,146 --> 00:24:00,048
What's your name?

431
00:24:00,849 --> 00:24:04,419
I'm Kim Jong Eun.

432
00:24:04,419 --> 00:24:05,920
Jong Eun.

433
00:24:05,920 --> 00:24:07,355
Can you cook?

434
00:24:08,623 --> 00:24:10,759
(Curious)

435
00:24:12,027 --> 00:24:13,862
Give me a recipe,

436
00:24:15,030 --> 00:24:16,598
and I can cook anything.

437
00:24:17,299 --> 00:24:18,933
Can everyone here cook?

438
00:24:19,534 --> 00:24:23,371
(Faced with Jong Eun's forceful question,)

439
00:24:23,371 --> 00:24:27,442
(everyone is at a loss for words.)

440
00:24:27,909 --> 00:24:29,811
Can you make things
like spicy braised chicken too?

441
00:24:29,811 --> 00:24:31,346
Yes, of course.

442
00:24:31,680 --> 00:24:33,448
One of the more difficult dishes...

443
00:24:33,448 --> 00:24:34,749
I've tried making is gnocchi.

444
00:24:34,749 --> 00:24:35,817
- Gnocchi?
- Yes.

445
00:24:36,618 --> 00:24:39,120
I'll make it for you if I get the chance.

446
00:24:40,121 --> 00:24:42,324
If we end up grocery shopping,

447
00:24:42,324 --> 00:24:44,025
we'll give you the perfect opportunity.

448
00:24:44,025 --> 00:24:44,893
For me?

449
00:24:44,893 --> 00:24:46,828
That's a little much.

450
00:24:46,828 --> 00:24:48,630
Now I feel pressured.

451
00:24:48,630 --> 00:24:51,566
(He quickly assumes a polite posture.)

452
00:24:54,369 --> 00:24:55,704
He's even dressed like a chef.

453
00:25:03,211 --> 00:25:05,513
Hello, I'm Kang Min Young.

454
00:25:05,814 --> 00:25:09,184
A potato-like guy with puppy-dog charm.

455
00:25:09,184 --> 00:25:12,621
I like guys who look like
they belong in the country.

456
00:25:12,621 --> 00:25:15,590
A handsome potato fresh from the dirt.

457
00:25:17,459 --> 00:25:18,660
After getting sick,

458
00:25:18,660 --> 00:25:20,462
I think I started looking for
someone dependable.

459
00:25:22,864 --> 00:25:26,534
And someone who appreciates
the little things.

460
00:25:32,374 --> 00:25:37,345
(The seventh housemate is Min Young.)

461
00:25:38,880 --> 00:25:40,282
Hello.

462
00:25:41,049 --> 00:25:42,617
Hello.

463
00:25:45,520 --> 00:25:46,588
Here's some water.

464
00:25:47,355 --> 00:25:48,523
Thank you.

465
00:25:49,924 --> 00:25:51,660
(Beams)

466
00:25:55,830 --> 00:25:57,632
What's your name?

467
00:25:57,632 --> 00:26:00,001
I'm Kang Min Young.

468
00:26:00,001 --> 00:26:01,403
Min Young.

469
00:26:04,539 --> 00:26:07,943
Wait, this isn't what I expected.

470
00:26:07,943 --> 00:26:09,511
They said everyone had been sick,

471
00:26:09,511 --> 00:26:13,148
but nobody there looked sick.

472
00:26:13,148 --> 00:26:15,617
Everyone must be doing really well now.

473
00:26:15,617 --> 00:26:17,085
That's what went through my mind.

474
00:26:17,719 --> 00:26:19,955
Are you nervous?

475
00:26:20,755 --> 00:26:21,890
No.

476
00:26:22,857 --> 00:26:24,059
Are you?

477
00:26:24,059 --> 00:26:25,260
- Me?
- Yes.

478
00:26:25,260 --> 00:26:26,828
I'm not that nervous either.

479
00:26:29,898 --> 00:26:31,099
Those two...

480
00:26:31,533 --> 00:26:33,201
Wait, are they sizing each other up?

481
00:26:33,201 --> 00:26:35,170
Why did that suddenly feel
a little confrontational?

482
00:26:35,170 --> 00:26:36,471
Do they have...

483
00:26:36,471 --> 00:26:37,739
some kind of rivalry going on?

484
00:26:37,739 --> 00:26:38,573
"No."

485
00:26:39,841 --> 00:26:42,510
(Jong Eun versus Min Young,
a sudden battle of wills?)

486
00:26:43,445 --> 00:26:45,814
In the past,

487
00:26:45,814 --> 00:26:49,184
my heart used to pound,

488
00:26:49,184 --> 00:26:52,253
but these days,

489
00:26:52,253 --> 00:26:53,922
it doesn't really race like that anymore.

490
00:26:54,756 --> 00:26:56,257
You seem fearless.

491
00:26:56,257 --> 00:26:57,192
Thank you.

492
00:26:57,192 --> 00:26:59,194
Jong Eun just made me laugh.

493
00:26:59,194 --> 00:27:01,529
He was so bright and cheerful.

494
00:27:01,529 --> 00:27:04,933
I wondered what illness he'd had.

495
00:27:04,933 --> 00:27:07,802
He seemed to be making an effort
to lighten the mood.

496
00:27:08,336 --> 00:27:10,171
As for Seon Ho,

497
00:27:11,339 --> 00:27:13,408
he didn't say much,

498
00:27:13,408 --> 00:27:16,077
so I thought he seemed very reserved.

499
00:27:16,077 --> 00:27:18,647
And I happen to like reserved people.

500
00:27:18,647 --> 00:27:20,081
Wait, I think...

501
00:27:20,081 --> 00:27:25,320
he might be about 30 percent potato.

502
00:27:25,320 --> 00:27:27,188
For now, I'm intrigued.

503
00:27:27,722 --> 00:27:30,892
Just a nicely scrubbed potato.

504
00:27:30,892 --> 00:27:32,294
A handsome potato.

505
00:27:33,461 --> 00:27:36,564
(The quietly smiling Seon Ho...)

506
00:27:36,865 --> 00:27:40,435
(has appeared
on Min Young's potato radar.)

507
00:27:43,471 --> 00:27:46,174
Hello, I'm Kim Na Young.

508
00:27:46,174 --> 00:27:48,476
I figured they could probably handle me.

509
00:27:48,977 --> 00:27:51,179
They've all overcome serious illnesses,

510
00:27:53,014 --> 00:27:55,750
so I thought I'd be easy by comparison.

511
00:28:00,355 --> 00:28:02,157
I've never experienced anything like that,

512
00:28:02,824 --> 00:28:06,361
but I'd love one dramatic encounter,
just once.

513
00:28:07,662 --> 00:28:08,830
When I date someone, I think,
"This is the one."

514
00:28:09,197 --> 00:28:12,233
That's how I want to approach love
from now on.

515
00:28:15,103 --> 00:28:21,610
(The eighth housemate is Na Young.)

516
00:28:22,210 --> 00:28:24,145
Hello.

517
00:28:24,145 --> 00:28:25,080
(Everyone bows.)

518
00:28:25,080 --> 00:28:27,716
- Hello.
- Hello.

519
00:28:28,984 --> 00:28:30,218
When I walked in,

520
00:28:30,218 --> 00:28:32,320
they were all sitting there
in a neat little row.

521
00:28:32,320 --> 00:28:33,888
And then they all...

522
00:28:33,888 --> 00:28:35,156
turned to look at me...

523
00:28:38,660 --> 00:28:40,895
one after another like dominoes.

524
00:28:40,895 --> 00:28:43,064
It was so adorable.

525
00:28:44,099 --> 00:28:46,601
Seeing that instantly put me
in a great mood.

526
00:28:46,601 --> 00:28:49,070
- Hello.
- Hello.

527
00:28:49,471 --> 00:28:51,106
(A delightful first meeting
that made Na Young smile)

528
00:28:51,106 --> 00:28:52,173
This must be my seat.

529
00:28:52,841 --> 00:28:54,643
You're the last one to arrive.

530
00:28:54,643 --> 00:28:56,144
Here's some water.

531
00:28:56,144 --> 00:28:57,245
Thank you.

532
00:28:58,046 --> 00:29:01,016
(With Na Young's arrival,
all eight are together.)

533
00:29:01,483 --> 00:29:02,951
How should I put this?

534
00:29:02,951 --> 00:29:05,420
Na Young feels like the team leader.

535
00:29:05,420 --> 00:29:06,488
(Agrees)

536
00:29:06,821 --> 00:29:08,089
Definitely team-leader energy.

537
00:29:08,089 --> 00:29:09,190
"All right, guys. Let's sit down."

538
00:29:09,190 --> 00:29:10,425
"It's okay. Have a seat."

539
00:29:10,825 --> 00:29:12,193
I think Ro Bin will be popular.

540
00:29:12,193 --> 00:29:13,194
- I agree.
- Right?

541
00:29:13,194 --> 00:29:15,797
Her outfit suited her so well.

542
00:29:15,797 --> 00:29:17,265
And I also think...

543
00:29:17,265 --> 00:29:19,434
Jong Eun has a lot of charm.

544
00:29:19,434 --> 00:29:21,202
He's kind of funny.

545
00:29:21,202 --> 00:29:23,305
He's a little awkward, but...

546
00:29:23,305 --> 00:29:24,773
He's confident.

547
00:29:24,773 --> 00:29:25,907
But he keeps missing little things.

548
00:29:26,541 --> 00:29:27,976
So he has this endearingly clumsy charm.

549
00:29:27,976 --> 00:29:29,678
Min Young and Jong Eun
were hilarious together too.

550
00:29:29,678 --> 00:29:30,745
Right, their chemistry.

551
00:29:30,745 --> 00:29:32,047
(Jong Eun and Min Young's
inexplicable rivalry)

552
00:29:32,047 --> 00:29:33,214
That power struggle.

553
00:29:33,214 --> 00:29:35,050
"No, I'm not nervous."

554
00:29:36,685 --> 00:29:38,019
I'm curious about their chemistry too.

555
00:29:38,687 --> 00:29:39,754
This is fascinating.

556
00:29:41,122 --> 00:29:43,458
Have you read that yet?

557
00:29:43,825 --> 00:29:45,460
We were waiting until everyone got here.

558
00:29:45,460 --> 00:29:46,828
- Really?
- Yes.

559
00:29:46,828 --> 00:29:48,163
I've been dying to read it.

560
00:29:54,436 --> 00:29:57,672
(House guide for their 7 days together)

561
00:29:59,874 --> 00:30:02,477
"On the first day,
introduce yourselves by name only."

562
00:30:03,211 --> 00:30:06,014
"Please gather together
to check the messages"...

563
00:30:06,014 --> 00:30:07,782
"delivered through the mailbox each day."

564
00:30:08,917 --> 00:30:12,354
"You may not exchange personal
social media or contact details."

565
00:30:12,354 --> 00:30:15,957
"Do not make a direct confession
before the final choice."

566
00:30:17,659 --> 00:30:20,929
"Give this time together
everything you've got."

567
00:30:21,830 --> 00:30:22,897
Those are the rules.

568
00:30:24,833 --> 00:30:25,600
That sounds good.

569
00:30:26,601 --> 00:30:28,203
(Time that had stood still...)

570
00:30:28,203 --> 00:30:31,106
(has begun to move again.)

571
00:30:33,241 --> 00:30:35,210
Should we get up?

572
00:30:35,210 --> 00:30:36,077
Do you want to look around the house?

573
00:30:36,077 --> 00:30:37,379
I think there was an upstairs too.

574
00:30:38,780 --> 00:30:39,781
No way.

575
00:30:41,683 --> 00:30:43,418
It's a terrace.

576
00:30:43,418 --> 00:30:45,553
This is so nice.

577
00:30:45,553 --> 00:30:46,521
No way.

578
00:30:46,521 --> 00:30:49,391
(The house has them
all excited at first sight.)

579
00:30:50,358 --> 00:30:53,662
(Second-floor terrace)

580
00:30:53,995 --> 00:30:56,998
(A place that will be filled
with honest conversations)

581
00:30:57,599 --> 00:30:59,100
(Second-floor terrace bar)

582
00:30:59,100 --> 00:31:00,168
Isn't it pretty?

583
00:31:00,168 --> 00:31:01,469
It is.

584
00:31:01,469 --> 00:31:02,404
This is amazing.

585
00:31:03,171 --> 00:31:05,707
It would be so pretty
to sit there at sunset.

586
00:31:06,708 --> 00:31:10,312
(A sweeping view of Seoul by night)

587
00:31:11,579 --> 00:31:14,482
(Ryun Gi and Seon Ho's room)

588
00:31:17,118 --> 00:31:18,853
(Do Gon and Jong Eun's room)

589
00:31:18,853 --> 00:31:19,888
It smells nice.

590
00:31:21,690 --> 00:31:22,624
There are so many vanities.

591
00:31:22,624 --> 00:31:23,992
This room is great.

592
00:31:24,259 --> 00:31:26,227
- Let's take this one.
- Yes, this is nice.

593
00:31:26,728 --> 00:31:27,562
(Women's room)

594
00:31:27,562 --> 00:31:28,897
This one's even better.

595
00:31:30,131 --> 00:31:31,032
Should we take this one?

596
00:31:31,032 --> 00:31:32,600
This should be ours.

597
00:31:32,600 --> 00:31:33,702
This has to be ours.

598
00:31:33,702 --> 00:31:35,170
We can sit there to do our makeup.

599
00:31:35,170 --> 00:31:37,872
Two of us can use this one,
and one can use that one.

600
00:31:37,872 --> 00:31:39,074
Is it already decided?

601
00:31:43,044 --> 00:31:47,115
(Playroom)

602
00:31:50,652 --> 00:31:54,723
(A space to unwind with music)

603
00:31:54,723 --> 00:31:56,524
There are vinyl records too.

604
00:31:56,524 --> 00:31:57,492
No way.

605
00:31:57,492 --> 00:31:59,160
Can we play them?

606
00:32:00,428 --> 00:32:02,230
- The turntable is over there.
- You're right.

607
00:32:02,230 --> 00:32:03,832
There are vinyl records.

608
00:32:03,832 --> 00:32:05,533
Do you listen to vinyl?

609
00:32:05,533 --> 00:32:06,801
Yeah.

610
00:32:07,836 --> 00:32:08,637
Are you into vinyl?

611
00:32:08,637 --> 00:32:10,105
- I love it.
- Really?

612
00:32:10,105 --> 00:32:12,774
- We can listen at night.
- Exactly.

613
00:32:12,774 --> 00:32:14,242
It'll be the perfect vibe.

614
00:32:14,242 --> 00:32:17,479
(A space that comes alive at night)

615
00:32:22,284 --> 00:32:26,388
(Living room)

616
00:32:26,888 --> 00:32:29,157
(Dining room)

617
00:32:29,157 --> 00:32:30,325
It's so pretty.

618
00:32:31,259 --> 00:32:32,193
Unbelievable.

619
00:32:34,062 --> 00:32:35,697
Is this where we eat?

620
00:32:35,697 --> 00:32:37,399
(A cozy dining area)

621
00:32:37,399 --> 00:32:38,366
It's so pretty.

622
00:32:40,335 --> 00:32:43,571
(Mailbox)

623
00:32:45,573 --> 00:32:47,976
(And another hidden room in the house)

624
00:32:47,976 --> 00:32:48,977
This is...

625
00:32:50,345 --> 00:32:51,079
What is it?

626
00:32:51,079 --> 00:32:52,948
It's locked. This room must be off-limits.

627
00:32:56,418 --> 00:33:00,422
(What will unfold beyond the locked door?)

628
00:33:07,862 --> 00:33:09,230
There must be something
inside the locked room.

629
00:33:09,230 --> 00:33:10,665
I guess there's something in there.

630
00:33:10,665 --> 00:33:12,100
What is it? What could it be?

631
00:33:12,100 --> 00:33:13,601
That looks like a clock ticking.

632
00:33:15,170 --> 00:33:16,071
You're right.

633
00:33:27,282 --> 00:33:29,451
(A breezy afternoon)

634
00:33:29,618 --> 00:33:32,854
(Jong Eun and Do Gon are unpacking.)

635
00:33:39,594 --> 00:33:44,032
(A pouch of medicine
is the first thing out of his bag.)

636
00:33:50,805 --> 00:33:54,576
(He puts his medicine
in the bedside drawer first.)

637
00:33:54,976 --> 00:33:57,545
Let's make the most of this week.

638
00:33:57,545 --> 00:33:58,413
Yes.

639
00:33:59,514 --> 00:34:00,515
I'm excited.

640
00:34:06,121 --> 00:34:08,757
(With something in hand,)

641
00:34:08,757 --> 00:34:12,861
(Jong Eun heads downstairs.)

642
00:34:13,561 --> 00:34:16,364
(Prep kitchen)

643
00:34:19,801 --> 00:34:22,804
(After putting an ice pack
in the freezer,)

644
00:34:25,573 --> 00:34:29,110
(he puts in eye drops.)

645
00:34:36,251 --> 00:34:38,286
They're what I'm using now.

646
00:34:38,286 --> 00:34:39,854
My serum eye drops.

647
00:34:39,854 --> 00:34:44,159
Serum eye drops are made
from your own blood,

648
00:34:44,426 --> 00:34:49,331
and they're very helpful
in healing corneal injuries.

649
00:34:50,832 --> 00:34:55,737
I have some pretty severe
lasting effects from chemotherapy.

650
00:34:55,737 --> 00:35:00,408
My eyes are dry,
my corneas are badly damaged,

651
00:35:00,408 --> 00:35:02,978
and my mucous membranes are in poor shape.

652
00:35:02,978 --> 00:35:07,616
The drops have to be refrigerated,

653
00:35:07,616 --> 00:35:12,220
so they feel cool going in,
almost like an ointment.

654
00:35:13,321 --> 00:35:16,491
It really feels like
they're healing the damage.

655
00:35:17,859 --> 00:35:19,628
(Each carrying the weight
of their daily lives,)

656
00:35:19,628 --> 00:35:23,231
(a new day begins.)

657
00:35:23,965 --> 00:35:26,635
(After everyone finishes unpacking...)

658
00:35:27,736 --> 00:35:30,138
Would anyone like coffee or tea?

659
00:35:30,138 --> 00:35:32,440
I thought it might be nice
to have tea together.

660
00:35:34,042 --> 00:35:36,077
(Tea time prepared by Do Gon)

661
00:35:36,077 --> 00:35:37,078
Thanks for making this.

662
00:35:37,078 --> 00:35:39,281
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

663
00:35:39,281 --> 00:35:40,815
Should we clink our cups?

664
00:35:40,815 --> 00:35:41,516
Clink our cups?

665
00:35:41,516 --> 00:35:43,785
Should we all clink our cups?

666
00:35:43,785 --> 00:35:44,986
Do we have to clink?

667
00:35:44,986 --> 00:35:46,488
(The most wholesome clinking in the world)

668
00:35:48,990 --> 00:35:49,891
This is delicious.

669
00:35:49,891 --> 00:35:50,925
It's really good.

670
00:35:50,925 --> 00:35:52,627
(A cup of tea melts away
the first-meeting nerves.)

671
00:35:52,627 --> 00:35:53,762
It's very unusual.

672
00:35:55,664 --> 00:35:56,831
Do Gon, this is delicious.

673
00:35:57,866 --> 00:35:58,700
It is, right?

674
00:35:59,134 --> 00:36:00,268
(Winks)

675
00:36:00,869 --> 00:36:03,772
(As everyone enjoys their tea,)

676
00:36:04,739 --> 00:36:05,740
(Startled)

677
00:36:06,207 --> 00:36:08,710
(a bell rings from somewhere.)

678
00:36:09,811 --> 00:36:12,614
Is that the mailbox?

679
00:36:12,614 --> 00:36:14,249
Should we go get it?

680
00:36:14,649 --> 00:36:15,383
Cool.

681
00:36:15,917 --> 00:36:21,122
(The first mail has arrived at the house.)

682
00:36:29,564 --> 00:36:30,598
We have mail.

683
00:36:32,267 --> 00:36:33,535
This is so cool.

684
00:36:35,070 --> 00:36:36,538
(Curious)

685
00:36:36,538 --> 00:36:39,674
Jong Eun, our brave one,
would you read it for us?

686
00:36:41,209 --> 00:36:42,110
All right.

687
00:36:46,014 --> 00:36:50,085
(The first mail delivered to the house)

688
00:36:52,504 --> 00:36:55,640
"This is a page from one
of the hardest chapters of your life."

689
00:36:55,994 --> 00:36:58,864
"Take turns reading yours
and sharing your stories."

690
00:36:59,752 --> 00:37:01,487
(Day 1)

691
00:37:01,487 --> 00:37:04,557
(This is a page from one
of the hardest chapters of your life.)

692
00:37:04,557 --> 00:37:07,593
(Take turns reading yours
and sharing your stories.)

693
00:37:15,935 --> 00:37:17,903
(Along with the message...)

694
00:37:17,903 --> 00:37:21,073
(came 8 envelopes.)

695
00:37:21,674 --> 00:37:23,142
Are they all different?

696
00:37:23,142 --> 00:37:24,176
This...

697
00:37:25,177 --> 00:37:26,445
I think so.

698
00:37:26,445 --> 00:37:27,813
There's a date.

699
00:37:28,481 --> 00:37:29,782
Do we each read our own?

700
00:37:29,782 --> 00:37:30,750
It looks like there's one for each of us.

701
00:37:30,750 --> 00:37:33,052
- Really? Is our name on it?
- No, there's a date.

702
00:37:33,052 --> 00:37:35,855
This one says July 2020.

703
00:37:36,355 --> 00:37:37,590
I think that's mine.

704
00:37:37,590 --> 00:37:38,724
- Is it?
- What?

705
00:37:38,724 --> 00:37:40,059
What does this date mean?

706
00:37:40,826 --> 00:37:42,928
July 2020.

707
00:37:43,529 --> 00:37:44,497
When I heard it,

708
00:37:45,331 --> 00:37:47,633
I knew it marked...

709
00:37:47,633 --> 00:37:49,702
a day I could never forget.

710
00:37:49,702 --> 00:37:51,370
I instinctively thought, "That's mine."

711
00:37:52,605 --> 00:37:54,774
"That has to be mine."

712
00:37:55,274 --> 00:37:57,009
November 2022.

713
00:37:57,376 --> 00:37:58,477
That's me.

714
00:37:58,477 --> 00:37:59,812
November 2022.

715
00:38:00,713 --> 00:38:03,916
It said November 2022.

716
00:38:03,916 --> 00:38:05,851
The moment I saw that year and month,

717
00:38:05,851 --> 00:38:08,387
I was instantly taken back.

718
00:38:09,722 --> 00:38:11,791
That was exactly when...

719
00:38:11,791 --> 00:38:14,627
I found out I was sick.

720
00:38:17,396 --> 00:38:18,664
She was so young.

721
00:38:22,368 --> 00:38:24,503
It's something you couldn't forget
even if you tried.

722
00:38:24,503 --> 00:38:25,671
Exactly.

723
00:38:27,406 --> 00:38:33,145
(A page from their battle-against-illness
diaries has arrived.)

724
00:38:39,185 --> 00:38:40,786
It felt like I was right back there,

725
00:38:40,786 --> 00:38:43,055
in the moment when I'd written it.

726
00:38:44,356 --> 00:38:47,293
Are these stories
from everyone else's treatment?

727
00:38:47,293 --> 00:38:49,128
I'd never heard firsthand...

728
00:38:49,395 --> 00:38:51,831
about what others went through
during treatment.

729
00:38:51,831 --> 00:38:54,567
I wondered what they'd thought
while going through that pain,

730
00:38:55,034 --> 00:38:57,670
and how they felt about it now.

731
00:38:58,003 --> 00:38:59,772
I was curious about all of those things.

732
00:39:04,744 --> 00:39:09,615
(Opening together the records
of years endured alone)

733
00:39:10,950 --> 00:39:12,051
Who should go first?

734
00:39:13,886 --> 00:39:14,720
Well,

735
00:39:15,688 --> 00:39:17,289
should we start with Ro Bin,

736
00:39:17,289 --> 00:39:18,524
or go the other way around?

737
00:39:18,524 --> 00:39:19,592
The other way, this time?

738
00:39:19,592 --> 00:39:20,760
No.

739
00:39:21,327 --> 00:39:23,429
(The battle to avoid going first begins.)

740
00:39:24,029 --> 00:39:24,897
No.

741
00:39:24,897 --> 00:39:25,831
Rock, paper, scissors.

742
00:39:25,831 --> 00:39:27,099
- Okay.
- Rock, paper, scissors, shoot.

743
00:39:27,099 --> 00:39:28,134
(Min Young chooses scissors.)

744
00:39:28,134 --> 00:39:30,035
(Ro Bin chooses rock.)

745
00:39:30,035 --> 00:39:32,438
(Ro Bin wins with rock.)

746
00:39:35,841 --> 00:39:37,243
Should I go first, Min Young?

747
00:39:37,243 --> 00:39:38,577
Then I'll go first.

748
00:39:39,645 --> 00:39:40,613
I'd like that.

749
00:39:42,681 --> 00:39:46,452
(Ro Bin finds the courage to go first.)

750
00:39:48,487 --> 00:39:49,488
I'll start reading.

751
00:39:52,625 --> 00:39:54,660
I'm so nervous.

752
00:39:54,660 --> 00:39:55,661
I'll read it.

753
00:40:00,399 --> 00:40:02,134
It was my first time revealing...

754
00:40:02,134 --> 00:40:04,904
my feelings...

755
00:40:04,904 --> 00:40:07,106
or what had been going through
my mind back then...

756
00:40:07,106 --> 00:40:10,042
in front of so many people,
so I was terrified.

757
00:40:10,042 --> 00:40:13,479
And they were such raw, honest emotions.

758
00:40:13,479 --> 00:40:15,548
I wondered what everyone would think...

759
00:40:15,548 --> 00:40:18,651
after hearing them.

760
00:40:19,518 --> 00:40:24,657
(Carefully revealing the story
buried deepest inside)

761
00:40:27,026 --> 00:40:29,128
It's been a long time
since I last wrote.

762
00:40:29,128 --> 00:40:32,131
The truth is, I haven't been okay.

763
00:40:32,131 --> 00:40:33,499
When the hospital told me
I needed chemotherapy...

764
00:40:33,499 --> 00:40:36,902
and had to prepare for a transplant,

765
00:40:36,902 --> 00:40:39,905
I was terrified,
and it felt like my heart was breaking.

766
00:40:42,641 --> 00:40:44,410
Will I make it out of here alive?

767
00:40:44,410 --> 00:40:48,214
I often can't control the emotions
that suddenly overwhelm me.

768
00:40:49,048 --> 00:40:51,050
Right now,
time moves more slowly for me...

769
00:40:51,050 --> 00:40:54,487
than a turtle or even a sloth.

770
00:40:57,523 --> 00:41:00,493
The physical pain I'll endure
during chemotherapy...

771
00:41:00,493 --> 00:41:02,294
doesn't scare me at all.

772
00:41:02,294 --> 00:41:03,996
What scares me is the hair loss
that will come with it...

773
00:41:03,996 --> 00:41:07,466
and how different I'll look
because of it.

774
00:41:07,466 --> 00:41:11,070
I'm terrified of facing
that version of myself.

775
00:41:18,811 --> 00:41:20,846
To get the bone marrow transplant,

776
00:41:20,846 --> 00:41:22,915
I was told I had to shave my head.

777
00:41:22,915 --> 00:41:24,049
But...

778
00:41:24,049 --> 00:41:26,652
the treatment itself
didn't scare me as much as...

779
00:41:26,652 --> 00:41:28,621
the idea of shaving my head.

780
00:41:28,821 --> 00:41:32,391
(Ro Bin during treatment)

781
00:41:38,931 --> 00:41:40,733
Gradually,

782
00:41:40,733 --> 00:41:41,901
I think my self-loathing got worse.

783
00:41:41,901 --> 00:41:44,537
I started seeing myself
as completely helpless.

784
00:41:44,537 --> 00:41:46,305
That was when...

785
00:41:46,672 --> 00:41:49,308
I began to hate myself more and more.

786
00:41:51,410 --> 00:41:56,549
(Time moved slowly for Ro Bin.)

787
00:42:02,621 --> 00:42:04,423
Both of my older sisters said...

788
00:42:04,423 --> 00:42:07,626
they'd do anything to save me,

789
00:42:07,626 --> 00:42:10,696
and rushed to the hospital
for blood tests without hesitation.

790
00:42:11,864 --> 00:42:14,333
In the examination room,
the doctor told us...

791
00:42:14,333 --> 00:42:16,769
my second-oldest sister wasn't a match,

792
00:42:16,769 --> 00:42:20,239
but my eldest sister was a perfect match.

793
00:42:26,846 --> 00:42:28,280
Rather than feeling happy,

794
00:42:28,280 --> 00:42:30,349
I felt incredibly grateful...

795
00:42:30,349 --> 00:42:32,384
and guilty toward my eldest sister.

796
00:42:34,286 --> 00:42:37,189
I received a bone marrow
transplant from her.

797
00:42:39,258 --> 00:42:43,929
I asked her, "How did you feel
when you donated to me?"

798
00:42:43,929 --> 00:42:45,297
And she said,

799
00:42:45,297 --> 00:42:47,099
"I never thought twice about it."

800
00:42:47,600 --> 00:42:50,669
"I had to save you."

801
00:42:50,669 --> 00:42:54,106
"There was nothing for me
to think about, Ro Bin."

802
00:42:54,106 --> 00:42:56,609
("There was nothing
to think about, Ro Bin.")

803
00:42:58,244 --> 00:43:01,280
Being family doesn't make that a given.

804
00:43:07,119 --> 00:43:08,687
Her sisters did so much for her.

805
00:43:10,556 --> 00:43:13,959
That couldn't have been an easy choice.

806
00:43:18,197 --> 00:43:19,932
That's what they drew
from my sister today.

807
00:43:23,536 --> 00:43:25,271
I look like a boy.

808
00:43:25,271 --> 00:43:26,338
No, you look like a girl.

809
00:43:26,338 --> 00:43:27,940
What part?

810
00:43:27,940 --> 00:43:29,408
- What?
- What part?

811
00:43:29,408 --> 00:43:30,276
Your eyes.

812
00:43:32,044 --> 00:43:35,781
My sisters took care of
every little thing for me...

813
00:43:35,781 --> 00:43:38,818
and constantly filled me
with words of certainty,

814
00:43:38,818 --> 00:43:42,221
like, "I know you're going to get better."

815
00:43:42,221 --> 00:43:43,155
Hearing that over and over made me think,

816
00:43:43,155 --> 00:43:45,524
"That's right.
I'm someone who can get better."

817
00:43:45,524 --> 00:43:46,625
I started believing it too.

818
00:43:48,761 --> 00:43:51,764
I will fight with everything I have
and hold on...

819
00:43:51,764 --> 00:43:54,266
so I can return to the family
who fought for me.

820
00:43:54,266 --> 00:43:56,035
This is only a passing storm.

821
00:43:56,035 --> 00:43:58,137
My illness will pass soon too.

822
00:44:00,139 --> 00:44:01,507
It's already spring.

823
00:44:01,507 --> 00:44:02,742
It's the weather
that brings things back to life.

824
00:44:03,576 --> 00:44:07,279
When the flowers bloom, I'll come back
with good news and write again.

825
00:44:07,980 --> 00:44:08,881
The end.

826
00:44:17,523 --> 00:44:18,524
You're strong.

827
00:44:20,392 --> 00:44:23,195
What exactly were you diagnosed with?

828
00:44:23,629 --> 00:44:26,766
My condition was a type of blood cancer...

829
00:44:26,766 --> 00:44:29,135
called myelodysplastic syndrome.

830
00:44:29,135 --> 00:44:30,469
It's also called MDS,

831
00:44:30,469 --> 00:44:33,239
and you can think of it
as a pre-leukemic condition.

832
00:44:33,239 --> 00:44:36,308
I was in the high-risk group,

833
00:44:36,308 --> 00:44:37,843
with a strong chance
of developing acute leukemia.

834
00:44:40,479 --> 00:44:41,981
Are you okay now?

835
00:44:41,981 --> 00:44:44,650
Yes, I've recovered a lot.

836
00:44:44,650 --> 00:44:46,752
This September marks three years.

837
00:44:50,122 --> 00:44:52,191
She came through it so well.

838
00:44:52,191 --> 00:44:53,426
Exactly.

839
00:44:55,861 --> 00:44:57,997
She's truly amazing.

840
00:44:57,997 --> 00:45:00,900
She worked so hard
to make it through all of that.

841
00:45:00,900 --> 00:45:03,569
All I could think was
how incredible they all were.

842
00:45:03,569 --> 00:45:05,137
They really went through so much.

843
00:45:08,908 --> 00:45:10,176
You did so well.

844
00:45:11,977 --> 00:45:14,013
You really did. You went through so much.

845
00:45:15,648 --> 00:45:18,417
After I finished reading,
I felt a weight lift.

846
00:45:18,951 --> 00:45:19,985
It made me realize...

847
00:45:20,719 --> 00:45:24,356
that expressing my emotions
wasn't as hard as I'd thought.

848
00:45:25,157 --> 00:45:27,860
And since...

849
00:45:27,860 --> 00:45:29,562
we'd all experienced illness,

850
00:45:29,562 --> 00:45:31,964
some things were easier to talk about.

851
00:45:33,466 --> 00:45:37,703
(Stories they can share
because they understand.)

852
00:45:39,138 --> 00:45:43,075
(Evening falls in shades of blue.)

853
00:45:45,411 --> 00:45:46,812
Should I go next?

854
00:45:59,525 --> 00:46:03,195
(September 2023,
Page two, Do Gon)

855
00:46:03,796 --> 00:46:05,531
Reading it again feels a little...

856
00:46:13,672 --> 00:46:15,307
Usually, in dramas or movies,

857
00:46:15,307 --> 00:46:17,410
when characters are told they have cancer,

858
00:46:18,811 --> 00:46:21,680
they sob and ask the doctor
if they'll live,

859
00:46:21,680 --> 00:46:23,082
then sometimes lash out in anger.

860
00:46:24,517 --> 00:46:26,485
But when I found myself
in that situation,

861
00:46:26,485 --> 00:46:28,387
I couldn't react at all.

862
00:46:31,457 --> 00:46:34,226
It felt like I'd been blindsided
by a full-force punch.

863
00:46:36,862 --> 00:46:37,763
"Why me?"

864
00:46:38,998 --> 00:46:41,133
My mind was filled with question marks.

865
00:46:41,634 --> 00:46:42,902
"What went wrong?"

866
00:46:44,036 --> 00:46:46,005
I simply couldn't accept it.

867
00:46:47,206 --> 00:46:49,508
The moment the doctor told me
I had cancer,

868
00:46:50,142 --> 00:46:52,044
it felt like he was saying,

869
00:46:52,044 --> 00:46:54,113
"You failed. It's over."

870
00:46:56,649 --> 00:46:58,417
What did I do wrong?

871
00:47:00,953 --> 00:47:03,322
I couldn't bring myself to call my family.

872
00:47:04,557 --> 00:47:06,225
I knew they'd worry about me...

873
00:47:07,927 --> 00:47:09,495
even more than I worried about myself.

874
00:47:10,763 --> 00:47:13,299
I couldn't bear the thought
of going home to an empty house,

875
00:47:15,301 --> 00:47:16,335
so I called...

876
00:47:23,209 --> 00:47:25,077
my close friends and acquaintances
to break the news...

877
00:47:25,077 --> 00:47:26,712
as I walked and talked for a long time.

878
00:47:28,914 --> 00:47:31,117
When people expressed concern
over the phone...

879
00:47:32,718 --> 00:47:33,519
Sorry.

880
00:47:49,301 --> 00:47:51,670
When people expressed their concern
over the phone,

881
00:47:51,670 --> 00:47:52,872
I told them I was fine,

882
00:47:53,639 --> 00:47:55,641
but I wasn't fine at all.

883
00:47:57,410 --> 00:47:59,011
I felt so wronged and angry.

884
00:48:04,683 --> 00:48:06,218
"Why did I get cancer?"

885
00:48:07,420 --> 00:48:09,955
I think I was simply angry
at the whole situation.

886
00:48:10,956 --> 00:48:13,259
I barely drink,

887
00:48:13,259 --> 00:48:16,195
and I exercised
and took care of myself every day.

888
00:48:17,329 --> 00:48:18,998
I don't even smoke.
I kept telling myself that,

889
00:48:18,998 --> 00:48:21,934
replaying the way I'd lived.

890
00:48:22,768 --> 00:48:24,970
(Do Gon before his illness)

891
00:48:25,638 --> 00:48:27,673
I'd volunteered whenever I could...

892
00:48:27,673 --> 00:48:29,208
and thought I'd lived
a good, diligent life.

893
00:48:29,208 --> 00:48:30,676
(Source: Compassion, photo by Heo Ho)

894
00:48:30,676 --> 00:48:34,080
I thought I'd lived a really healthy life.

895
00:48:35,948 --> 00:48:37,049
So why me?

896
00:48:38,384 --> 00:48:42,321
(Do Gon looked back on the life he'd led.)

897
00:48:43,022 --> 00:48:46,592
Things got hard once treatment began.

898
00:48:47,693 --> 00:48:50,463
One day, I was washing my hair...

899
00:48:50,463 --> 00:48:53,866
when I saw clumps coming out in my hands.

900
00:48:54,667 --> 00:48:56,035
I thought, "So it's starting."

901
00:49:02,141 --> 00:49:03,476
"I'm a patient now too."

902
00:49:09,448 --> 00:49:10,716
It's like being in a car accident.

903
00:49:12,885 --> 00:49:16,021
There's no reason. It's not your fault,

904
00:49:16,021 --> 00:49:17,690
and it isn't anyone else's either.

905
00:49:22,895 --> 00:49:24,897
On the way home from the hospital,

906
00:49:25,598 --> 00:49:26,966
I walked and walked.

907
00:49:34,440 --> 00:49:36,509
The weather was so clear and warm,

908
00:49:37,309 --> 00:49:38,844
but tears streamed from my eyes.

909
00:49:40,446 --> 00:49:42,715
That's it.

910
00:49:46,786 --> 00:49:48,187
When were you sick?

911
00:49:49,522 --> 00:49:51,857
September 2023.

912
00:49:53,058 --> 00:49:55,194
It's been...

913
00:49:56,295 --> 00:49:57,997
two years since I finished treatment.

914
00:49:57,997 --> 00:50:00,766
Just over two years.

915
00:50:00,766 --> 00:50:01,901
What kind of...

916
00:50:02,401 --> 00:50:05,638
Mine was also a blood cancer.

917
00:50:05,638 --> 00:50:10,576
I was diagnosed with stage two
diffuse large B-cell lymphoma.

918
00:50:10,576 --> 00:50:12,445
I've heard of it too.

919
00:50:13,112 --> 00:50:15,347
I had three rounds of chemotherapy...

920
00:50:15,347 --> 00:50:17,850
and 15 rounds of radiation therapy.

921
00:50:17,850 --> 00:50:20,019
That's a lot of radiation.

922
00:50:20,019 --> 00:50:21,921
How many rounds?

923
00:50:21,921 --> 00:50:22,988
Fifteen.

924
00:50:26,859 --> 00:50:29,595
I had radiation twice, and it was so hard.

925
00:50:29,595 --> 00:50:31,230
How did you do it fifteen times?

926
00:50:31,464 --> 00:50:33,065
Exactly. 15 times is so...

927
00:50:35,134 --> 00:50:37,603
No matter how hard I tried to hold it in,

928
00:50:37,603 --> 00:50:40,005
I couldn't control my emotions.

929
00:50:40,873 --> 00:50:44,176
At the same time, it was cathartic,

930
00:50:44,176 --> 00:50:47,580
like a weight had been
lifted off my chest.

931
00:50:48,781 --> 00:50:50,416
When I go home now...

932
00:50:50,416 --> 00:50:52,218
(Do Gon looks visibly more at ease.)

933
00:50:52,218 --> 00:50:56,155
Honestly, when I first got here,
everyone looked so...

934
00:50:56,155 --> 00:50:58,057
- Healthy.
- healthy.

935
00:50:58,057 --> 00:50:59,125
Honestly.

936
00:50:59,125 --> 00:51:00,159
(Agrees)

937
00:51:00,159 --> 00:51:01,961
I honestly wondered
if anyone here had been sick.

938
00:51:01,961 --> 00:51:03,162
Exactly.

939
00:51:03,162 --> 00:51:05,264
Nobody looks sick at all.

940
00:51:05,264 --> 00:51:07,466
And everyone here is healthy now.

941
00:51:07,466 --> 00:51:09,702
We're all so strong.

942
00:51:09,702 --> 00:51:11,570
We're built like warriors.

943
00:51:11,570 --> 00:51:13,105
Seriously, nobody looks sick.

944
00:51:15,241 --> 00:51:17,276
They look so much more relaxed.

945
00:51:17,276 --> 00:51:18,911
They look completely relaxed now.

946
00:51:18,911 --> 00:51:21,480
Instead of just saying,
"Hi, I'm Jung Yong Hwa,"

947
00:51:21,480 --> 00:51:24,717
or "Here are the things I like,"

948
00:51:24,717 --> 00:51:26,819
this lets them connect
on a much deeper level.

949
00:51:29,688 --> 00:51:33,659
(A time to get to know
one another more deeply)

950
00:51:36,028 --> 00:51:36,996
Don't cry.

951
00:51:37,463 --> 00:51:39,432
Our brave Jong Eun.

952
00:51:40,633 --> 00:51:41,767
(July 2023,
Page three, Jong Eun)

953
00:51:41,767 --> 00:51:42,701
Shall we?

954
00:51:43,936 --> 00:51:47,039
(Now, it's brave Jong Eun's turn.)

955
00:51:50,843 --> 00:51:54,447
I take an early bus
to the hospital again today.

956
00:51:55,147 --> 00:51:57,016
It's very foggy.

957
00:51:57,483 --> 00:51:59,685
I wonder
how this blood test will turn out.

958
00:52:00,119 --> 00:52:01,620
They've drawn so much blood...

959
00:52:01,620 --> 00:52:04,723
that getting a needle
doesn't faze me anymore.

960
00:52:41,293 --> 00:52:43,129
I thought the bone marrow transplant...

961
00:52:43,129 --> 00:52:44,864
would mean...

962
00:52:48,300 --> 00:52:49,335
my treatment was over.

963
00:52:55,374 --> 00:52:58,310
But life afterward...

964
00:52:59,745 --> 00:53:02,448
feels even more grueling
because of the aftereffects.

965
00:53:09,822 --> 00:53:13,359
Lately, my elbow has hurt so much...

966
00:53:13,359 --> 00:53:15,361
that I can't even lift my arm.

967
00:53:16,095 --> 00:53:18,330
My fingers feel like
they're slowly stiffening,

968
00:53:21,400 --> 00:53:23,769
and my hands keep getting weaker.

969
00:53:24,737 --> 00:53:27,473
Even when I try to drink water,
my hands are so weak...

970
00:53:27,473 --> 00:53:30,976
that I keep dropping things
and need both hands to pour it.

971
00:53:33,779 --> 00:53:34,814
No.

972
00:53:35,548 --> 00:53:36,682
That's heartbreaking.

973
00:53:45,558 --> 00:53:47,960
I want my old body back.

974
00:53:49,161 --> 00:53:53,232
Now I feel like I have the body
of an 80-year-old.

975
00:53:54,667 --> 00:53:58,237
I used to take real pride in my fitness.

976
00:53:58,237 --> 00:54:01,640
Running, futsal, and soccer.

977
00:54:01,640 --> 00:54:03,442
I was good at all of them.

978
00:54:04,243 --> 00:54:05,644
(Jong Eun was fit enough
to serve in the Marine Corps.)

979
00:54:05,644 --> 00:54:08,547
I also joined the Marines
because I wanted to get stronger.

980
00:54:09,582 --> 00:54:12,151
I think I did very well in the military.

981
00:54:13,319 --> 00:54:15,955
I was great at training,

982
00:54:15,955 --> 00:54:19,725
and I think I was always
among the top five on runs.

983
00:54:21,560 --> 00:54:26,499
I think I was even more drawn
to things others avoided.

984
00:54:26,966 --> 00:54:31,570
I thought that would make life
more rewarding and fulfilling.

985
00:54:32,605 --> 00:54:36,809
(Jong Eun dreamed of saving lives.)

986
00:54:39,145 --> 00:54:41,380
I earned my Level 1 EMT certification...

987
00:54:42,214 --> 00:54:47,486
and spent a year studying
for the firefighter written exam.

988
00:54:47,486 --> 00:54:51,590
But then, unfortunately, I got sick,

989
00:54:51,590 --> 00:54:53,659
and had no choice but to give it up.

990
00:54:53,893 --> 00:54:55,895
(Jong Eun during treatment)

991
00:54:55,895 --> 00:54:57,763
Not being able to become a firefighter...

992
00:54:59,031 --> 00:55:00,566
hit me the hardest.

993
00:55:01,534 --> 00:55:03,569
It had been my one and only goal,

994
00:55:04,437 --> 00:55:06,238
but because of this illness,

995
00:55:06,238 --> 00:55:08,908
I had to give up my dream.

996
00:55:09,308 --> 00:55:11,310
That was incredibly hard to accept.

997
00:55:13,946 --> 00:55:15,314
That must've felt so unfair.

998
00:55:15,981 --> 00:55:18,884
You have to be incredibly fit
to train as a firefighter.

999
00:55:20,086 --> 00:55:22,354
The things we were good at,

1000
00:55:22,354 --> 00:55:24,156
the things we loved,

1001
00:55:24,690 --> 00:55:28,861
and the things we once took for granted.

1002
00:55:28,861 --> 00:55:31,530
Losing the ability to do those things
can hurt more than being sick.

1003
00:55:32,098 --> 00:55:32,998
That's right.

1004
00:55:44,143 --> 00:55:45,077
It's okay.

1005
00:56:00,526 --> 00:56:02,194
I'd dreamed of it since I was young.

1006
00:56:03,028 --> 00:56:04,430
To become a firefighter,

1007
00:56:05,331 --> 00:56:06,732
I studied hard...

1008
00:56:07,666 --> 00:56:09,335
and even joined the Marine Corps.

1009
00:56:10,636 --> 00:56:12,538
The uncertain future scares me.

1010
00:56:13,305 --> 00:56:16,509
When will I finally be cured?

1011
00:56:33,125 --> 00:56:34,293
I'm sorry.

1012
00:56:34,293 --> 00:56:35,227
Don't be.

1013
00:56:43,202 --> 00:56:46,906
I have acute myeloid leukemia.

1014
00:56:48,774 --> 00:56:52,178
Going through this illness,

1015
00:56:53,312 --> 00:56:55,548
I came to see it
as two separate challenges.

1016
00:56:55,548 --> 00:56:56,615
The first...

1017
00:56:58,184 --> 00:57:00,686
was treatment to eliminate the cancer.

1018
00:57:01,587 --> 00:57:04,957
The second was life after treatment.

1019
00:57:06,158 --> 00:57:07,693
My doctor told me...

1020
00:57:07,693 --> 00:57:09,895
that my prognosis...

1021
00:57:10,596 --> 00:57:13,499
would be very poor.

1022
00:57:14,467 --> 00:57:16,035
After your first round of chemotherapy,

1023
00:57:17,169 --> 00:57:19,205
you're supposed to go into remission.

1024
00:57:19,405 --> 00:57:21,207
But I didn't go into remission at all.

1025
00:57:21,674 --> 00:57:25,778
(Remission,
when cancer cells decrease or disappear)

1026
00:57:25,778 --> 00:57:28,080
Since chemotherapy couldn't get rid
of the cancer cells,

1027
00:57:28,080 --> 00:57:29,982
the doctors said
we'd try a bone marrow transplant...

1028
00:57:29,982 --> 00:57:33,385
to eliminate them all.

1029
00:57:33,953 --> 00:57:35,821
So I had the transplant,

1030
00:57:36,489 --> 00:57:38,124
and as a result,

1031
00:57:38,824 --> 00:57:42,161
I'm now living
with graft-versus-host disease.

1032
00:57:43,028 --> 00:57:45,498
When it attacks specific organs,

1033
00:57:45,498 --> 00:57:48,701
what kinds of complications...

1034
00:57:48,701 --> 00:57:50,069
are there?

1035
00:57:51,370 --> 00:57:54,640
I understand the symptoms vary widely
from person to person.

1036
00:57:54,640 --> 00:57:56,208
For me, right now,

1037
00:57:56,208 --> 00:58:00,679
my lung function
has declined significantly.

1038
00:58:00,679 --> 00:58:05,084
As I mentioned, my fingers and joints...

1039
00:58:05,084 --> 00:58:07,153
are becoming stiff.

1040
00:58:09,855 --> 00:58:12,892
I also have dry eyes, and my saliva...

1041
00:58:12,892 --> 00:58:14,427
I don't produce saliva anymore.

1042
00:58:14,427 --> 00:58:16,695
My tear glands were damaged too,
so I can't cry.

1043
00:58:16,996 --> 00:58:18,464
That's why you need artificial tears.

1044
00:58:18,464 --> 00:58:21,700
Normally, I'd be crying my eyes out
reading this,

1045
00:58:22,535 --> 00:58:25,037
but no tears come.

1046
00:58:25,638 --> 00:58:28,441
New medications keep coming out,

1047
00:58:28,441 --> 00:58:31,677
so we're monitoring my progress
on a new drug.

1048
00:58:32,578 --> 00:58:33,979
You've been through so much.

1049
00:58:34,346 --> 00:58:35,714
You really have.

1050
00:58:39,452 --> 00:58:40,386
Stay strong.

1051
00:58:45,591 --> 00:58:50,162
Jong Eun was reading his...

1052
00:58:50,162 --> 00:58:51,764
right before me,

1053
00:58:52,298 --> 00:58:58,137
and we'd had the same illness...

1054
00:58:58,137 --> 00:59:01,874
and gone through almost the same journey,

1055
00:59:01,874 --> 00:59:04,910
so his pain felt
that much more real to me.

1056
00:59:05,978 --> 00:59:10,382
Once I started crying, I couldn't stop.

1057
00:59:12,017 --> 00:59:12,985
You can do it.

1058
00:59:13,385 --> 00:59:14,720
(December 2018,
Page four, Ye Ji)

1059
00:59:14,720 --> 00:59:16,422
I'm barely managing to hold it together.

1060
00:59:18,758 --> 00:59:20,726
It's okay to cry.

1061
00:59:20,726 --> 00:59:21,694
Exactly. So what if you cry?

1062
00:59:21,694 --> 00:59:24,230
Don't hold it in. Let it all out.

1063
00:59:24,463 --> 00:59:25,798
Bottling it up only makes it worse.

1064
00:59:40,179 --> 00:59:41,313
Give me a moment.

1065
00:59:42,648 --> 00:59:44,016
Take all the time you need.

1066
00:59:46,018 --> 00:59:47,953
Take your time, Ye Ji. There's no rush.

1067
00:59:59,098 --> 01:00:00,800
Do you need some water?

1068
01:00:03,002 --> 01:00:04,003
I'll get you some.

1069
01:00:12,411 --> 01:00:16,849
(Seon Ho pours water for Ye Ji.)

1070
01:00:20,319 --> 01:00:21,253
Thank you.

1071
01:00:30,496 --> 01:00:31,464
I'll try to read it.

1072
01:00:35,234 --> 01:00:36,936
It's been a few days since I last wrote.

1073
01:00:37,803 --> 01:00:39,739
My first stay in an isolation room...

1074
01:00:39,739 --> 01:00:41,707
was nothing like I'd imagined.

1075
01:00:42,308 --> 01:00:45,111
The sound of someone being rushed
to the treatment room,

1076
01:00:45,111 --> 01:00:48,981
of someone sobbing in pain
during a bone marrow test,

1077
01:00:48,981 --> 01:00:51,350
and of 'Code Blue' blaring
over the speakers,

1078
01:00:52,818 --> 01:00:54,820
and the silence that fell over every meal.

1079
01:00:55,788 --> 01:00:59,091
All of it took a heavy mental toll on me.

1080
01:00:59,091 --> 01:01:00,559
Everything was unfamiliar.

1081
01:01:04,497 --> 01:01:08,501
Of all days,
Grandma was visiting instead of Mom.

1082
01:01:08,501 --> 01:01:10,436
Suddenly, my blood pressure dropped,

1083
01:01:10,436 --> 01:01:13,572
and I developed a fever
as my throat and mouth began to hurt.

1084
01:01:13,572 --> 01:01:17,076
My oxygen saturation fell,
so they put me on oxygen.

1085
01:01:21,213 --> 01:01:23,115
I guess things hadn't
actually gotten better.

1086
01:01:23,115 --> 01:01:24,784
Take your time.

1087
01:01:25,618 --> 01:01:26,519
Slowly.

1088
01:01:31,490 --> 01:01:34,493
She probably hasn't been able
to let herself cry all this time.

1089
01:01:43,402 --> 01:01:46,272
My body was tangled in tubes and wires.

1090
01:01:47,440 --> 01:01:51,310
When Grandma arrived and saw me,
she burst into tears.

1091
01:01:51,310 --> 01:01:53,212
I remember telling her,

1092
01:02:09,662 --> 01:02:11,564
"Don't cry."

1093
01:02:12,298 --> 01:02:15,067
I was so scared...

1094
01:02:16,869 --> 01:02:17,837
that I lost all sense of time,

1095
01:02:17,837 --> 01:02:21,273
but the nurse told me
I just had to make it through the night.

1096
01:02:22,041 --> 01:02:25,444
(Ye Ji during treatment)

1097
01:02:26,512 --> 01:02:28,314
I don't remember that day at all.

1098
01:02:28,314 --> 01:02:30,950
For almost five days, I was...

1099
01:02:30,950 --> 01:02:33,486
in what I guess you'd call a semi-coma.

1100
01:02:35,888 --> 01:02:38,724
When I finally opened my eyes,
I thought, "Where am I?"

1101
01:02:38,724 --> 01:02:42,361
I'd forgotten I was in a hospital.

1102
01:02:42,361 --> 01:02:44,463
It took me a while
to figure out where I was.

1103
01:02:45,331 --> 01:02:48,667
But somehow,
I miraculously pulled through.

1104
01:02:52,071 --> 01:02:55,841
That was when the real fight began.

1105
01:02:55,841 --> 01:02:58,344
There was no knowing when I'd get better.

1106
01:02:59,145 --> 01:03:02,548
(The 4 to 5 years of treatment
that followed)

1107
01:03:03,449 --> 01:03:07,286
I'm terrified of fighting an illness
with no end in sight.

1108
01:03:07,286 --> 01:03:09,688
Will there ever come a time
when I can finally breathe easy?

1109
01:03:12,324 --> 01:03:15,060
Will I live every day
trembling with fear?

1110
01:03:17,630 --> 01:03:19,465
I know I can live each day
to the fullest...

1111
01:03:19,465 --> 01:03:21,300
as though it were my last.

1112
01:03:21,300 --> 01:03:23,903
Can't I have just one more chance?

1113
01:03:27,239 --> 01:03:28,474
You did so well.

1114
01:03:39,251 --> 01:03:40,186
I was also...

1115
01:03:41,353 --> 01:03:45,057
diagnosed with acute myeloid leukemia.

1116
01:03:46,892 --> 01:03:50,529
I was first diagnosed in December 2018.

1117
01:03:50,529 --> 01:03:53,399
During chemotherapy,

1118
01:03:54,433 --> 01:03:57,336
I truly came close to dying.

1119
01:03:58,604 --> 01:04:02,341
I heard later
that my doctor had told my mom...

1120
01:04:02,341 --> 01:04:06,612
there was little hope for me.

1121
01:04:07,446 --> 01:04:10,149
So I spent around four or five years
in treatment.

1122
01:04:10,950 --> 01:04:12,184
Four or five years.

1123
01:04:13,486 --> 01:04:17,456
I'd spent so many years
fighting the illness.

1124
01:04:27,099 --> 01:04:31,070
I related deeply to what Jong Eun said.

1125
01:04:31,070 --> 01:04:33,139
It felt like I'd been given
an old woman's body.

1126
01:04:33,139 --> 01:04:35,274
- Someone in her eighties.
- Yes.

1127
01:04:35,274 --> 01:04:38,244
I may look perfectly healthy
on the outside,

1128
01:04:38,244 --> 01:04:39,645
but on the inside...

1129
01:04:39,645 --> 01:04:41,380
Exactly.

1130
01:04:41,380 --> 01:04:42,782
Only you know what it's really like.

1131
01:04:44,850 --> 01:04:46,886
So that's why you cried.

1132
01:04:46,886 --> 01:04:49,488
It caught me completely off guard.

1133
01:04:51,056 --> 01:04:53,793
You truly went through so much.

1134
01:05:00,599 --> 01:05:02,101
She really did.

1135
01:05:02,101 --> 01:05:03,769
She went through so much.

1136
01:05:03,769 --> 01:05:06,238
In a way,

1137
01:05:07,206 --> 01:05:10,976
they'd all been carrying
something enormous inside...

1138
01:05:10,976 --> 01:05:14,413
and working so hard to keep it hidden.

1139
01:05:14,413 --> 01:05:16,615
I choked up watching them too.

1140
01:05:16,816 --> 01:05:21,120
But as they shared these stories,

1141
01:05:21,120 --> 01:05:23,589
Ye Ji could relate to Jong Eun,

1142
01:05:23,589 --> 01:05:27,126
and they connected over
what they'd been through.

1143
01:05:27,126 --> 01:05:29,261
Watching that made me feel...

1144
01:05:29,261 --> 01:05:32,498
that they could comfort
and support each other there.

1145
01:05:42,341 --> 01:05:43,909
As I listened to everyone's stories,

1146
01:05:43,909 --> 01:05:45,411
I recognized every emotion they described.

1147
01:05:46,345 --> 01:05:48,814
"I remember feeling this way,
and then that way."

1148
01:05:52,318 --> 01:05:53,652
"Right. That's exactly how it was."

1149
01:05:56,255 --> 01:05:57,456
Do you think this will be too hard?

1150
01:05:57,456 --> 01:05:59,558
No. I'm okay. I think I can do it.

1151
01:06:00,359 --> 01:06:02,828
(July 2020,
Page five, Seon Ho)

1152
01:06:08,267 --> 01:06:09,568
I'll give it a try.

1153
01:06:13,472 --> 01:06:16,308
While serving on active duty,

1154
01:06:16,308 --> 01:06:19,111
I became a stage-four cancer patient.

1155
01:06:25,351 --> 01:06:26,852
I underwent tests every day,

1156
01:06:27,586 --> 01:06:29,488
and every time
the military doctor made rounds,

1157
01:06:29,488 --> 01:06:31,457
my results were worse.

1158
01:06:33,192 --> 01:06:35,861
One day, I had pain in my side,
so they ran some tests.

1159
01:06:36,796 --> 01:06:38,731
They said the disease
was progressing too quickly...

1160
01:06:39,331 --> 01:06:40,900
and that something unknown...

1161
01:06:40,900 --> 01:06:43,169
was literally dissolving my bones.

1162
01:06:44,070 --> 01:06:48,207
I'd heard of bones breaking,

1163
01:06:50,276 --> 01:06:53,112
but that was the first time
I'd heard of them dissolving.

1164
01:06:54,113 --> 01:06:56,382
I couldn't tell my parents.

1165
01:06:57,149 --> 01:06:58,918
They already had so much
on their shoulders,

1166
01:06:59,785 --> 01:07:02,254
and I felt like I'd become
another burden.

1167
01:07:02,254 --> 01:07:04,857
I was so sorry to make them worry.

1168
01:07:05,658 --> 01:07:08,327
I didn't know what to say,

1169
01:07:08,327 --> 01:07:10,129
how they would react,

1170
01:07:12,231 --> 01:07:14,366
or what their faces would look like.

1171
01:07:15,468 --> 01:07:19,071
(Seon Ho and his father
before his illness)

1172
01:07:19,872 --> 01:07:21,373
I think I just felt so sorry.

1173
01:07:21,373 --> 01:07:22,675
At the time,

1174
01:07:22,675 --> 01:07:25,845
my family was going through
a difficult period.

1175
01:07:26,378 --> 01:07:28,347
I felt like I was placing...

1176
01:07:30,049 --> 01:07:33,753
yet another burden on them.

1177
01:07:37,857 --> 01:07:41,527
I knew this would break
my parents' hearts,

1178
01:07:41,527 --> 01:07:42,895
and that weighed heavily on me.

1179
01:07:43,662 --> 01:07:45,397
That's why I couldn't bring myself
to tell them.

1180
01:07:50,636 --> 01:07:53,906
It hurts that he couldn't tell them
because they'd worry.

1181
01:07:55,441 --> 01:07:58,043
He saw himself as a burden.

1182
01:07:58,043 --> 01:07:59,178
Even though he was the one who was sick.

1183
01:08:06,419 --> 01:08:09,889
I asked the military doctor
if the worst could happen.

1184
01:08:11,056 --> 01:08:13,259
In other words, I asked if I could die.

1185
01:08:14,193 --> 01:08:17,463
He said, "We can't rule anything out."

1186
01:08:20,166 --> 01:08:21,967
It felt like the sky was falling.

1187
01:08:25,071 --> 01:08:27,006
I called my parents that day.

1188
01:08:33,079 --> 01:08:37,149
My mother cried and apologized, saying,

1189
01:08:37,149 --> 01:08:39,518
"If I'd taken better care of you,"

1190
01:08:40,052 --> 01:08:41,187
"you never would've
gotten sick like this."

1191
01:08:55,000 --> 01:08:57,536
I kept telling her I was fine...

1192
01:08:58,471 --> 01:09:00,973
because I was afraid crying
would make her worry,

1193
01:09:01,774 --> 01:09:03,609
but the truth was,
I was crying because I was terrified.

1194
01:09:05,678 --> 01:09:06,846
She just cried.

1195
01:09:06,846 --> 01:09:08,214
My mom kept saying how sorry she was,

1196
01:09:09,548 --> 01:09:11,250
saying she felt like my illness
was all her fault.

1197
01:09:14,987 --> 01:09:16,789
Hearing that...

1198
01:09:16,789 --> 01:09:18,290
made me feel like I was
falling apart all over again.

1199
01:09:22,194 --> 01:09:23,662
My dad had a really hard time too.

1200
01:09:25,097 --> 01:09:26,565
He could barely speak.

1201
01:09:27,199 --> 01:09:28,768
He was silent for a long time,

1202
01:09:30,169 --> 01:09:31,337
then told me he wasn't worried about me.

1203
01:09:31,337 --> 01:09:33,072
I think that's what he said back then.

1204
01:09:34,206 --> 01:09:36,242
(It's Dad. Stay strong, Seon Ho.)

1205
01:09:36,242 --> 01:09:39,178
(My dear son Seon Ho,
we didn't get to talk yesterday.)

1206
01:09:39,178 --> 01:09:41,647
(Did you sleep well through the night?)

1207
01:09:41,647 --> 01:09:43,883
(I just got off work.)

1208
01:09:43,883 --> 01:09:46,318
(We were born into this world
and became father and son.)

1209
01:09:46,318 --> 01:09:48,187
(Could we ever be given
such a bond again?)

1210
01:09:48,187 --> 01:09:50,556
(The bond between us
is not meant to be broken yet.)

1211
01:09:50,556 --> 01:09:52,958
(Our time has not yet come.)

1212
01:09:52,958 --> 01:09:55,327
(You must overcome this,
whatever it takes.)

1213
01:09:55,561 --> 01:10:00,299
When I pictured
my parents' faces after I died,

1214
01:10:00,299 --> 01:10:01,600
I realized I could never do that to them.

1215
01:10:02,134 --> 01:10:04,437
So I gritted my teeth and steeled myself.

1216
01:10:04,437 --> 01:10:07,139
I wasn't going down that easily.

1217
01:10:10,509 --> 01:10:13,312
But my condition kept getting worse,

1218
01:10:14,180 --> 01:10:16,082
and the pain spread throughout my body.

1219
01:10:16,082 --> 01:10:19,151
Even opioid painkillers stopped working.

1220
01:10:21,053 --> 01:10:23,255
There were so many moments
of utter despair.

1221
01:10:24,056 --> 01:10:25,624
It was incredibly hard.

1222
01:10:27,226 --> 01:10:28,594
After that day, the pain made it hard...

1223
01:10:28,594 --> 01:10:30,563
even to get out of bed.

1224
01:10:31,097 --> 01:10:33,899
My fever rose and fell
two or three times a day.

1225
01:10:34,600 --> 01:10:38,437
My throat swelled
and began to change color.

1226
01:10:38,437 --> 01:10:40,439
Even moving my fingers became difficult.

1227
01:10:41,040 --> 01:10:43,242
I thought, "I really might die."

1228
01:10:50,716 --> 01:10:53,452
The people in my hospital room...

1229
01:10:53,452 --> 01:10:54,920
made a promise to one another.

1230
01:10:54,920 --> 01:10:57,556
We'd all get well soon
and go out for a beer.

1231
01:10:58,557 --> 01:11:02,261
There were three of us, including me,
who made that promise.

1232
01:11:02,261 --> 01:11:04,096
But I was the only one who survived.

1233
01:11:22,181 --> 01:11:24,183
When I was sick as a child,

1234
01:11:24,183 --> 01:11:28,053
I watched my parents
and family around me...

1235
01:11:28,053 --> 01:11:30,623
break down and suffer.

1236
01:11:30,623 --> 01:11:32,558
Ever since I was young, I felt guilty,

1237
01:11:32,558 --> 01:11:36,262
as if everything were my fault.

1238
01:11:36,262 --> 01:11:40,132
I remember feeling that way.

1239
01:11:40,132 --> 01:11:42,234
So watching that, I really related to him.

1240
01:11:42,435 --> 01:11:45,037
My parents were like that too.

1241
01:11:45,037 --> 01:11:48,774
With my hospital bills piling up,

1242
01:11:48,774 --> 01:11:50,910
they would always go out...

1243
01:11:50,910 --> 01:11:54,580
to sell gimbap and help pay for them.

1244
01:11:54,580 --> 01:11:55,981
Even as a little kid, I noticed it,

1245
01:11:55,981 --> 01:11:58,184
and I still remember it vividly.

1246
01:11:58,184 --> 01:12:01,053
Ye Na, you must relate to him even more.

1247
01:12:09,061 --> 01:12:10,062
You went through so much.

1248
01:12:12,431 --> 01:12:13,365
You did.

1249
01:12:16,469 --> 01:12:17,570
I had...

1250
01:12:17,570 --> 01:12:20,639
anaplastic large cell lymphoma,

1251
01:12:20,639 --> 01:12:22,742
which is a type of blood cancer.

1252
01:12:22,742 --> 01:12:25,544
I was told it's actually quite rare.

1253
01:12:25,544 --> 01:12:29,148
If you look it up,
there's very little information.

1254
01:12:30,850 --> 01:12:33,319
And how are you doing now?

1255
01:12:33,319 --> 01:12:36,589
Last year marked five years for me.

1256
01:12:37,356 --> 01:12:39,391
I went to the hospital,

1257
01:12:39,391 --> 01:12:42,528
and you know how outpatient reports
are usually covered in notes?

1258
01:12:43,829 --> 01:12:45,898
"You've been through a lot.
You don't need to come back."

1259
01:12:45,898 --> 01:12:47,533
Mine was simply that.

1260
01:12:49,301 --> 01:12:50,236
Unbelievable.

1261
01:12:51,337 --> 01:12:54,273
I didn't know what to feel in that moment.

1262
01:12:55,241 --> 01:12:57,710
I thought to myself,
"You've been through so much,"

1263
01:12:57,710 --> 01:12:59,512
and also, "What a battle that was."

1264
01:13:01,947 --> 01:13:03,182
Congratulations.

1265
01:13:03,182 --> 01:13:04,383
Five years.

1266
01:13:04,383 --> 01:13:05,818
Seriously.

1267
01:13:05,818 --> 01:13:07,486
I can't imagine how that must've felt.

1268
01:13:07,486 --> 01:13:08,754
He cried more than anyone.

1269
01:13:10,856 --> 01:13:12,458
He cried the most.
You didn't see him, right?

1270
01:13:14,226 --> 01:13:15,394
He was crying so hard.

1271
01:13:16,729 --> 01:13:18,364
Those words, "You've been through a lot."

1272
01:13:19,064 --> 01:13:20,232
Those words got to me too.

1273
01:13:32,978 --> 01:13:36,949
(Why Ryun Gi was especially tearful)

1274
01:13:38,668 --> 01:13:41,905
Those words,
"You've been through a lot"...

1275
01:13:43,873 --> 01:13:47,077
felt like someone finally acknowledged
all I'd been through,

1276
01:13:47,077 --> 01:13:48,244
and like it was finally over.

1277
01:13:49,546 --> 01:13:52,048
I wanted so badly to hear those words too.

1278
01:13:52,048 --> 01:13:54,417
I think that's why I reacted that way.

1279
01:14:21,077 --> 01:14:22,112
Why?

1280
01:14:23,079 --> 01:14:24,180
Who is it?

1281
01:14:26,783 --> 01:14:28,284
Who's making you feel so sad?

1282
01:14:29,119 --> 01:14:30,220
Don't cry.

1283
01:14:31,621 --> 01:14:33,490
(Concerned)

1284
01:14:35,759 --> 01:14:37,060
Did it remind you of when you were sick?

1285
01:14:40,196 --> 01:14:41,898
Everyone's just so incredible.

1286
01:14:42,532 --> 01:14:43,600
So are you.

1287
01:14:45,402 --> 01:14:46,636
You should be proud.

1288
01:14:55,045 --> 01:14:57,147
It's so nice to have everyone there...

1289
01:14:57,147 --> 01:14:58,748
comforting one another.

1290
01:15:00,183 --> 01:15:01,518
And cheering each other on.

1291
01:15:07,123 --> 01:15:08,591
You should go around bragging about it.

1292
01:15:08,591 --> 01:15:09,626
(Laughter)

1293
01:15:09,626 --> 01:15:10,660
Seriously.

1294
01:15:12,228 --> 01:15:13,563
All...

1295
01:15:13,563 --> 01:15:15,265
the difficult and painful moments...

1296
01:15:15,265 --> 01:15:17,867
they went through...

1297
01:15:17,867 --> 01:15:21,037
hit especially close to home
for me right now.

1298
01:15:22,472 --> 01:15:24,874
I think that's why I couldn't stop crying.

1299
01:15:26,710 --> 01:15:29,512
(November 2025,
Page six, Ryun Gi)

1300
01:15:30,046 --> 01:15:31,548
It was a beautiful day,

1301
01:15:31,548 --> 01:15:35,452
the kind when no worries came to mind...

1302
01:15:35,452 --> 01:15:37,821
and the fall foliage was stunning.

1303
01:15:38,788 --> 01:15:40,523
At 4 a.m.,

1304
01:15:40,523 --> 01:15:42,726
I suddenly woke up.

1305
01:15:42,726 --> 01:15:44,527
I felt so nauseated
I thought I might throw up,

1306
01:15:44,527 --> 01:15:46,429
my head was spinning,

1307
01:15:46,429 --> 01:15:48,064
and I was drenched in a cold sweat.

1308
01:15:50,767 --> 01:15:52,669
I thought the room might be stuffy,

1309
01:15:52,669 --> 01:15:54,471
so I opened my bedroom door,

1310
01:15:56,206 --> 01:15:58,541
then collapsed and blacked out.

1311
01:16:01,011 --> 01:16:04,581
I didn't seem to be getting
any better all weekend,

1312
01:16:04,581 --> 01:16:05,682
so I went to the hospital.

1313
01:16:07,083 --> 01:16:09,652
When I described my symptoms
to my doctor,

1314
01:16:09,652 --> 01:16:12,555
they scolded me for not going
to the ER after fainting...

1315
01:16:12,555 --> 01:16:15,659
and for waiting so long
to come to the hospital.

1316
01:16:16,593 --> 01:16:18,261
I looked at the doctor,

1317
01:16:18,261 --> 01:16:20,363
and the doctor looked so grave.

1318
01:16:21,631 --> 01:16:24,134
They couldn't seem to get the words out.

1319
01:16:24,134 --> 01:16:26,870
They just kept sighing.

1320
01:16:26,870 --> 01:16:29,673
After a long pause, the doctor said,

1321
01:16:29,673 --> 01:16:32,642
"I'm so sorry to have to tell you this."

1322
01:16:32,642 --> 01:16:36,279
"You're so young,
and I'm sorry I have to say it."

1323
01:16:36,279 --> 01:16:37,547
And then,

1324
01:16:38,815 --> 01:16:42,385
the doctor said, "It's cancer."

1325
01:16:44,554 --> 01:16:47,857
The tumor was very close to my esophagus,

1326
01:16:47,857 --> 01:16:50,894
so they said my entire stomach
had to be removed.

1327
01:16:56,433 --> 01:16:59,235
An elderly patient
in the bed across from mine said,

1328
01:17:00,203 --> 01:17:03,139
"You need to eat
everything you want right now."

1329
01:17:03,139 --> 01:17:05,475
Those words stayed with me.

1330
01:17:06,643 --> 01:17:08,945
As soon as I heard that,
I left the hospital...

1331
01:17:08,945 --> 01:17:10,647
and had my last supper.

1332
01:17:12,515 --> 01:17:14,884
A Mom's Touch White Garlic Burger Combo,

1333
01:17:14,884 --> 01:17:16,986
cheese fries and a soda.

1334
01:17:16,986 --> 01:17:18,421
That sounds delicious.

1335
01:17:22,459 --> 01:17:24,627
That's so specific.

1336
01:17:28,565 --> 01:17:30,300
That still wasn't enough,

1337
01:17:30,300 --> 01:17:32,736
so I went to Baskin-Robbins
and ate a whole pint by myself.

1338
01:17:35,405 --> 01:17:37,574
It would be such a shame
if that were my last meal.

1339
01:17:38,775 --> 01:17:41,478
Why did something like this
have to happen to me?

1340
01:17:43,380 --> 01:17:46,216
But the more I thought about it,

1341
01:17:48,618 --> 01:17:50,553
the more I began to blame myself.

1342
01:17:50,553 --> 01:17:53,556
I'd spent my whole life
pushing myself relentlessly,

1343
01:17:53,556 --> 01:17:56,559
working so hard I'd worn down my own body.

1344
01:17:56,559 --> 01:17:59,929
So I felt like it was my fault.

1345
01:18:01,665 --> 01:18:03,633
(Ryun Gi in high school)

1346
01:18:04,234 --> 01:18:08,738
I studied relentlessly in high school.

1347
01:18:08,738 --> 01:18:13,009
There were times I studied
on less than four hours of sleep.

1348
01:18:13,009 --> 01:18:16,479
Thanks to my hard work and a little luck,

1349
01:18:16,479 --> 01:18:18,848
I got into a university in the Seoul area.

1350
01:18:19,683 --> 01:18:22,352
As a freshman,

1351
01:18:22,352 --> 01:18:24,287
I became my department's
class representative,

1352
01:18:24,287 --> 01:18:26,089
or what we usually call
the department rep.

1353
01:18:26,690 --> 01:18:30,794
Before I knew it, I was president
of my college's student council.

1354
01:18:31,761 --> 01:18:34,464
I guess one thing
just kept leading to another.

1355
01:18:34,964 --> 01:18:36,599
Even after I started working,

1356
01:18:37,467 --> 01:18:38,902
I was always...

1357
01:18:39,736 --> 01:18:40,937
pushing myself...

1358
01:18:42,972 --> 01:18:45,742
and trying to excel at everything.

1359
01:18:45,742 --> 01:18:48,712
I thought I'd finally make some money,
find my own hobbies,

1360
01:18:48,712 --> 01:18:52,282
and start the second chapter of my life.

1361
01:18:53,683 --> 01:18:55,819
I really did end up living a second life,
didn't I?

1362
01:18:57,120 --> 01:18:58,988
I'd only just landed a job.

1363
01:18:59,989 --> 01:19:02,359
I was finally going to have some fun.

1364
01:19:03,393 --> 01:19:05,528
Life can feel so daunting, can't it?

1365
01:19:06,196 --> 01:19:08,164
Life really isn't easy.

1366
01:19:09,666 --> 01:19:11,167
That's all I wrote.

1367
01:19:18,074 --> 01:19:19,342
I tried to write it on a lighter note.

1368
01:19:21,911 --> 01:19:22,912
That's really...

1369
01:19:23,780 --> 01:19:25,081
admirable.

1370
01:19:25,682 --> 01:19:27,917
I don't think I could've written that way.

1371
01:19:29,719 --> 01:19:31,654
What exactly were you diagnosed with?

1372
01:19:33,957 --> 01:19:35,125
I have...

1373
01:19:36,026 --> 01:19:38,294
stomach cancer.

1374
01:19:42,532 --> 01:19:44,534
The tumor was in a difficult spot,

1375
01:19:44,534 --> 01:19:46,903
so I had a total gastrectomy.

1376
01:19:46,903 --> 01:19:51,541
My esophagus and small intestine...

1377
01:19:51,541 --> 01:19:53,743
are now connected directly.

1378
01:19:53,743 --> 01:19:55,679
Last year,

1379
01:19:56,513 --> 01:20:00,316
I was diagnosed in November
and had surgery the very next month.

1380
01:20:01,184 --> 01:20:03,453
It all happened incredibly fast.

1381
01:20:03,987 --> 01:20:05,355
So that was only recently.

1382
01:20:05,355 --> 01:20:06,423
It really was very recent.

1383
01:20:06,423 --> 01:20:08,058
- Wasn't it last year?
- Last year?

1384
01:20:08,058 --> 01:20:09,092
Last November.

1385
01:20:09,626 --> 01:20:10,994
I see.

1386
01:20:10,994 --> 01:20:14,798
I'm actually still
undergoing chemotherapy.

1387
01:20:15,365 --> 01:20:18,935
I've had four of eight rounds so far,

1388
01:20:19,502 --> 01:20:21,404
and I'm receiving adjuvant chemotherapy.

1389
01:20:21,404 --> 01:20:26,409
Right now happens to be
when I feel my best.

1390
01:20:26,676 --> 01:20:29,112
Right after this, I'll have to go back...

1391
01:20:29,112 --> 01:20:31,614
and receive another round.

1392
01:20:31,981 --> 01:20:34,217
So honestly,
I'm still having a really hard time.

1393
01:20:34,417 --> 01:20:37,520
It's hard even now,
and as I listened earlier,

1394
01:20:37,520 --> 01:20:38,955
I kept thinking about
what I'm going through.

1395
01:20:43,927 --> 01:20:46,296
Everyone here has been so incredible.

1396
01:20:46,763 --> 01:20:47,998
No wonder you cried.

1397
01:20:55,705 --> 01:20:56,840
At such a difficult time.

1398
01:20:56,840 --> 01:20:59,075
But I really think you'll beat this.

1399
01:20:59,075 --> 01:20:59,976
That's right.

1400
01:20:59,976 --> 01:21:01,611
You're a man.

1401
01:21:04,481 --> 01:21:06,449
You were bursting with confidence earlier.

1402
01:21:06,449 --> 01:21:07,717
Exactly. I'm sure of it.

1403
01:21:08,618 --> 01:21:09,552
You'll beat it.

1404
01:21:09,552 --> 01:21:10,653
Thank you.

1405
01:21:16,226 --> 01:21:22,165
The moment I realized I could
no longer share good food with people,

1406
01:21:22,666 --> 01:21:24,501
I was...

1407
01:21:24,501 --> 01:21:26,436
overwhelmed with regret and sadness.

1408
01:21:26,436 --> 01:21:28,571
Even down the road,

1409
01:21:28,805 --> 01:21:30,874
I probably won't be able to eat
the way I used to.

1410
01:21:33,076 --> 01:21:34,477
Right now,

1411
01:21:35,278 --> 01:21:37,113
I'm craving spicy food so badly.

1412
01:21:39,149 --> 01:21:40,216
Super spicy noodles.

1413
01:21:42,552 --> 01:21:43,853
I really want some spicy noodles.

1414
01:21:46,656 --> 01:21:47,824
You've truly been through so much.

1415
01:21:47,824 --> 01:21:48,958
You really have.

1416
01:21:57,534 --> 01:21:58,535
I'm so curious.

1417
01:21:59,536 --> 01:22:00,770
I'm really curious.

1418
01:22:00,770 --> 01:22:02,205
I'm genuinely so curious.

1419
01:22:02,205 --> 01:22:03,273
You're curious?

1420
01:22:03,273 --> 01:22:06,443
(July 2023,
Page seven, Na Young)

1421
01:22:08,778 --> 01:22:11,514
Not long ago,
I lost my hearing in one ear.

1422
01:22:16,519 --> 01:22:19,322
Assuming it was just fatigue,
I went to an ENT specialist.

1423
01:22:19,322 --> 01:22:20,490
After the tests,

1424
01:22:20,490 --> 01:22:22,158
they said it wasn't sudden hearing loss.

1425
01:22:22,158 --> 01:22:24,694
They suspected a tumor
and recommended an MRI.

1426
01:22:27,597 --> 01:22:30,500
I hoped they were wrong,
but it was a brain tumor.

1427
01:22:30,500 --> 01:22:33,003
A little demon was sitting
on my auditory nerve.

1428
01:22:33,003 --> 01:22:35,638
The tumor was right where
several nerves converge.

1429
01:22:35,638 --> 01:22:37,574
I was told removing it surgically
could cause paralysis...

1430
01:22:37,574 --> 01:22:39,609
or even blindness.

1431
01:22:40,310 --> 01:22:42,112
The only way to cure this tumor...

1432
01:22:42,112 --> 01:22:44,247
is to perform a craniotomy...

1433
01:22:44,247 --> 01:22:46,082
and remove it completely.

1434
01:22:47,150 --> 01:22:51,421
But the potential complications
from surgery are severe.

1435
01:22:51,421 --> 01:22:55,191
So we decided to avoid surgery
if at all possible...

1436
01:22:55,725 --> 01:23:00,697
and focus only on shrinking the tumor.

1437
01:23:01,564 --> 01:23:04,067
Does that mean
I have to live with this forever?

1438
01:23:04,067 --> 01:23:07,003
(Na Young during treatment)

1439
01:23:07,537 --> 01:23:09,773
I looked in the mirror,

1440
01:23:09,773 --> 01:23:12,042
and the face staring back wasn't mine.

1441
01:23:12,042 --> 01:23:13,943
My skin was breaking out,

1442
01:23:13,943 --> 01:23:15,512
and my hair was falling out.

1443
01:23:16,680 --> 01:23:20,650
As a woman, it was impossible
not to care about how I looked.

1444
01:23:20,650 --> 01:23:24,020
(Na Young before her illness)

1445
01:23:24,020 --> 01:23:26,723
I changed a lot after that.

1446
01:23:26,723 --> 01:23:28,858
(Illness made her a stranger to herself.)

1447
01:23:29,292 --> 01:23:30,827
That was when I cried for the first time.

1448
01:23:31,861 --> 01:23:32,962
I was scared.

1449
01:23:34,864 --> 01:23:38,001
They said I could lose all hearing
in my left ear,

1450
01:23:38,001 --> 01:23:39,803
so I began steroid treatment.

1451
01:23:39,803 --> 01:23:43,039
The side effects made my body
and face swell rapidly.

1452
01:23:43,039 --> 01:23:44,841
My body doesn't feel like my own.

1453
01:23:44,841 --> 01:23:46,443
Even a short walk
leaves me out of breath.

1454
01:23:46,443 --> 01:23:49,512
My hands and legs swell
until I can't even see the bones.

1455
01:23:50,613 --> 01:23:51,948
During my time in the hospital,

1456
01:23:51,948 --> 01:23:54,217
I kept thinking about death.

1457
01:23:57,587 --> 01:23:59,289
Looking back,
I don't think I've lived my life...

1458
01:23:59,289 --> 01:24:01,157
doing everything I truly wanted to do.

1459
01:24:02,425 --> 01:24:04,594
From now on, I'll do everything I want.

1460
01:24:05,095 --> 01:24:07,330
I'll learn whatever I want
and travel wherever I want.

1461
01:24:07,330 --> 01:24:09,599
I'll have all the fun I want
and live life to the fullest.

1462
01:24:09,599 --> 01:24:11,301
I'll follow my heart
with the precious time I have,

1463
01:24:11,301 --> 01:24:13,837
loving, working, playing, and living well.

1464
01:24:14,304 --> 01:24:15,372
That's all.

1465
01:24:20,243 --> 01:24:24,748
I have a brain tumor
called a vestibular schwannoma.

1466
01:24:25,382 --> 01:24:29,819
It's in a particularly delicate location,

1467
01:24:29,819 --> 01:24:31,488
so surgery would come with...

1468
01:24:31,488 --> 01:24:34,524
an extremely high risk of complications.

1469
01:24:34,524 --> 01:24:36,226
I can't have surgery,

1470
01:24:36,226 --> 01:24:39,829
so for now, we're just trying to keep
the tumor from growing...

1471
01:24:39,829 --> 01:24:42,198
because I'm still so young.

1472
01:24:42,198 --> 01:24:45,001
But if we did that, this ear would...

1473
01:24:45,001 --> 01:24:47,704
So now I can't hear out of it at all.

1474
01:24:47,704 --> 01:24:48,972
Your left ear?

1475
01:24:48,972 --> 01:24:51,041
Yes, I can't hear anything
in my left ear now.

1476
01:24:51,041 --> 01:24:53,076
The doctor actually put me on steroids...

1477
01:24:53,076 --> 01:24:57,414
to try to save my hearing,

1478
01:24:57,414 --> 01:24:59,416
but the treatment was...

1479
01:24:59,416 --> 01:25:00,417
incredibly difficult for me.

1480
01:25:00,417 --> 01:25:02,919
Steroids really put your body
through a lot.

1481
01:25:02,919 --> 01:25:05,989
I gained 20 kilograms
in less than a month...

1482
01:25:05,989 --> 01:25:07,724
just from the swelling.

1483
01:25:07,724 --> 01:25:08,792
For me,
it was actually the exact opposite.

1484
01:25:08,792 --> 01:25:10,460
That's the opposite
of what happened to me.

1485
01:25:10,460 --> 01:25:11,861
Don't steroids make you hungrier?

1486
01:25:11,861 --> 01:25:15,665
I was practically doing
a food show back then.

1487
01:25:15,665 --> 01:25:17,200
I'd eat an entire pizza by myself.

1488
01:25:19,602 --> 01:25:20,970
A whole pizza?

1489
01:25:20,970 --> 01:25:22,872
That sounds so good.

1490
01:25:24,107 --> 01:25:25,508
(Flustered)

1491
01:25:25,508 --> 01:25:26,676
Wait a second.

1492
01:25:28,912 --> 01:25:30,246
I want pizza so badly right now.

1493
01:25:30,246 --> 01:25:31,081
Why are you doing this?

1494
01:25:31,081 --> 01:25:32,916
I want Domino's pizza.

1495
01:25:34,884 --> 01:25:36,353
(Everyone is flustered.)

1496
01:25:36,353 --> 01:25:37,787
- Wait a second.
- Hold on.

1497
01:25:38,621 --> 01:25:40,090
He's hungry. He wants some right now.

1498
01:25:40,090 --> 01:25:42,125
We can't talk about food like that.

1499
01:25:42,125 --> 01:25:43,059
Ryun Gi.

1500
01:25:43,059 --> 01:25:44,027
He can't have it.

1501
01:25:44,027 --> 01:25:46,396
We don't know whether to laugh or not.

1502
01:25:47,263 --> 01:25:48,732
He does have a sense of humor.

1503
01:25:49,432 --> 01:25:51,267
(An awkward moment)

1504
01:25:54,137 --> 01:25:55,305
Shall we move on?

1505
01:25:55,705 --> 01:25:58,074
I don't have much to share.

1506
01:25:58,074 --> 01:25:59,109
I'll go.

1507
01:25:59,109 --> 01:26:00,410
(January 2016,
The final page, Min Young)

1508
01:26:03,179 --> 01:26:04,647
But don't look at me, okay?

1509
01:26:05,248 --> 01:26:08,818
(Min Young's adorable request
before going last)

1510
01:26:08,818 --> 01:26:10,487
Then let's all do this.

1511
01:26:13,289 --> 01:26:16,226
(Pretending not to look)

1512
01:26:18,128 --> 01:26:20,497
After my second round of chemotherapy,
I had surgery.

1513
01:26:20,497 --> 01:26:22,298
Before the operation, my doctor said...

1514
01:26:22,298 --> 01:26:24,968
I might wake up with one leg amputated.

1515
01:26:27,437 --> 01:26:29,239
If that was going to happen,
wouldn't I be better off dead?

1516
01:26:29,239 --> 01:26:31,374
I said that and got scolded by my mom.

1517
01:26:32,342 --> 01:26:35,311
They made a 14-centimeter incision
across my knee...

1518
01:26:35,311 --> 01:26:37,247
and implanted an artificial bone.

1519
01:26:37,247 --> 01:26:39,249
The surgery left a huge scar on my leg,

1520
01:26:39,249 --> 01:26:41,184
and they said I'd never be able
to bend my knee again.

1521
01:26:42,919 --> 01:26:44,521
How am I going to wear
my school uniform?

1522
01:26:44,521 --> 01:26:46,556
I love wearing short skirts too,

1523
01:26:46,556 --> 01:26:48,692
but it looks like I'll only be able
to wear pants now.

1524
01:26:49,192 --> 01:26:51,828
The exact date it all began was...

1525
01:26:51,828 --> 01:26:54,364
in January, when I was 14,

1526
01:26:54,364 --> 01:26:55,532
the day before my birthday.

1527
01:26:56,166 --> 01:26:59,469
I had late-stage osteosarcoma.

1528
01:27:00,303 --> 01:27:04,007
It was in my left knee,

1529
01:27:04,007 --> 01:27:06,943
and because it looked so concerning,

1530
01:27:06,943 --> 01:27:08,678
they removed a large section of bone.

1531
01:27:10,246 --> 01:27:12,582
It was about 14 centimeters.

1532
01:27:14,551 --> 01:27:17,354
I thought, "This is it. I'm going to die."

1533
01:27:17,954 --> 01:27:20,757
"I'm only 14, and I'm going to die."

1534
01:27:22,759 --> 01:27:24,227
She was only in seventh grade.

1535
01:27:24,227 --> 01:27:25,762
She was so young.

1536
01:27:26,262 --> 01:27:28,365
- She must've been terrified.
- Exactly.

1537
01:27:28,665 --> 01:27:31,001
I honestly can't imagine it.

1538
01:27:31,001 --> 01:27:32,902
None of us has ever been through
anything like that.

1539
01:27:32,902 --> 01:27:34,637
And she was so young.

1540
01:27:34,637 --> 01:27:36,239
I can't even begin
to imagine how she felt.

1541
01:27:38,041 --> 01:27:42,145
I heard osteosarcoma is known
for its grueling chemotherapy.

1542
01:27:42,145 --> 01:27:45,615
Even if I barely ran a brush
through my hair,

1543
01:27:45,615 --> 01:27:47,050
it would fall out in clumps.

1544
01:27:49,219 --> 01:27:51,888
I started wearing a wig when I was 14.

1545
01:27:55,859 --> 01:27:57,927
It wasn't just the hair on my head.
I lost all my body hair too.

1546
01:27:57,927 --> 01:28:00,397
I had no eyebrows or eyelashes either.

1547
01:28:01,131 --> 01:28:01,931
A high bun.

1548
01:28:01,931 --> 01:28:04,167
I wanted so badly to wear a high bun
with my uniform,

1549
01:28:04,167 --> 01:28:06,136
but I never got to do it.

1550
01:28:07,837 --> 01:28:10,607
All I wanted was to wear my uniform
with a high bun or ponytail.

1551
01:28:10,607 --> 01:28:12,709
I guess that was my dream.

1552
01:28:13,643 --> 01:28:15,078
They said my hair might never grow back.

1553
01:28:15,078 --> 01:28:16,312
I'm scared the boys
will see me without hair...

1554
01:28:16,312 --> 01:28:18,581
and make fun of me.

1555
01:28:20,417 --> 01:28:22,185
I want to wear my hair in a high bun...

1556
01:28:22,185 --> 01:28:23,586
and have smooth, healthy hair.

1557
01:28:23,586 --> 01:28:26,990
I wanted to run around with my friends,
my straight hair flowing.

1558
01:28:29,693 --> 01:28:33,029
Could I go on living
if my hair never grows back?

1559
01:28:35,231 --> 01:28:37,334
Will I be able to date?
What about marriage?

1560
01:28:39,402 --> 01:28:42,072
It hurts so much that there are
so many things I never got to try.

1561
01:28:44,174 --> 01:28:48,345
"I'm only a kid. Why did I get cancer?"
Whenever I say that, Mom tells me,

1562
01:28:48,345 --> 01:28:49,913
"It's because you're so special,"

1563
01:28:49,913 --> 01:28:52,582
"and you're strong enough to overcome it."

1564
01:28:52,582 --> 01:28:54,584
Whenever Mom says that,

1565
01:28:54,584 --> 01:28:57,721
I get so angry, but when I think about it,
maybe she's right.

1566
01:28:57,921 --> 01:28:59,856
One moment, I think I can beat this,

1567
01:28:59,856 --> 01:29:01,624
and the next,
I think I'd be better off dead.

1568
01:29:01,624 --> 01:29:03,593
My feelings keep swinging back and forth.

1569
01:29:03,593 --> 01:29:04,894
That's where it ends.

1570
01:29:10,633 --> 01:29:15,672
Osteosarcoma is a cancer
that forms in the bones.

1571
01:29:15,672 --> 01:29:19,576
Because it's uncommon in Korea
and classified as a rare cancer,

1572
01:29:19,909 --> 01:29:21,778
I had many surgeries.

1573
01:29:21,778 --> 01:29:24,180
I had about nine rounds of chemotherapy.

1574
01:29:24,180 --> 01:29:27,283
Treatment lasted about a year,
and I had surgery...

1575
01:29:28,018 --> 01:29:29,185
three or four times.

1576
01:29:29,185 --> 01:29:32,689
I think I had three or four
on my leg alone.

1577
01:29:32,889 --> 01:29:35,759
But that was so long ago now,
almost ten years.

1578
01:29:36,226 --> 01:29:37,627
Recently,

1579
01:29:38,061 --> 01:29:40,196
although they never formally
declared me cured,

1580
01:29:40,196 --> 01:29:42,432
they told me, "You only need
to come back if you want to."

1581
01:29:42,432 --> 01:29:43,867
So my treatment is now over.

1582
01:29:45,435 --> 01:29:46,770
You've been through so much.

1583
01:29:49,973 --> 01:29:51,908
Most people don't think of walking...

1584
01:29:51,908 --> 01:29:54,711
as anything special, but I do.

1585
01:29:54,711 --> 01:29:56,646
Whenever I see people walking,

1586
01:29:56,646 --> 01:29:58,048
I want to tell them...

1587
01:29:58,048 --> 01:30:00,316
that they have two incredible legs.

1588
01:30:05,455 --> 01:30:10,860
(The past now preserved on a single page)

1589
01:30:11,094 --> 01:30:12,395
Did all of these entries...

1590
01:30:12,395 --> 01:30:15,832
come from diaries, blogs, or social media?

1591
01:30:15,832 --> 01:30:18,101
Mine was a diary.

1592
01:30:18,101 --> 01:30:20,403
I wrote mine on a blog.

1593
01:30:20,403 --> 01:30:21,638
Mine was a diary too.

1594
01:30:21,638 --> 01:30:24,607
After everything we've been through,

1595
01:30:24,607 --> 01:30:27,444
I think we all felt
the need to document it.

1596
01:30:27,444 --> 01:30:28,845
That's right.

1597
01:30:28,845 --> 01:30:30,447
The truth is,
none of us knows what might happen.

1598
01:30:30,447 --> 01:30:32,148
Sometimes I wrote
as if I were writing my will.

1599
01:30:32,148 --> 01:30:33,683
I did too.

1600
01:30:33,683 --> 01:30:34,851
And...

1601
01:30:34,851 --> 01:30:37,187
there's very little information out there
for young patients.

1602
01:30:37,187 --> 01:30:39,189
I was also...

1603
01:30:39,189 --> 01:30:42,125
very active on my blog at first.

1604
01:30:42,125 --> 01:30:43,727
There just wasn't enough information,

1605
01:30:43,727 --> 01:30:45,495
especially for younger patients.

1606
01:30:45,495 --> 01:30:47,697
So I started writing in December 2018.

1607
01:30:47,697 --> 01:30:51,267
I've kept documenting my story ever since.

1608
01:30:51,568 --> 01:30:53,470
So I feel...

1609
01:30:54,337 --> 01:30:57,273
there must be a reason I was spared,

1610
01:30:57,741 --> 01:31:00,910
and I try to put as much
helpful information out there as I can.

1611
01:31:01,344 --> 01:31:03,947
Which hospitals did you all go to?

1612
01:31:03,947 --> 01:31:06,449
I went to Seoul National University
Bundang Hospital.

1613
01:31:06,449 --> 01:31:07,951
I went
to Pusan National University Hospital.

1614
01:31:08,818 --> 01:31:10,420
That's pretty far.

1615
01:31:11,254 --> 01:31:12,689
Where did you go?

1616
01:31:12,689 --> 01:31:14,090
Guess where I went.

1617
01:31:14,090 --> 01:31:15,291
What?

1618
01:31:15,291 --> 01:31:17,327
What is this, an MBTI or something?

1619
01:31:17,327 --> 01:31:19,529
I went to Seoul St. Mary's Hospital.

1620
01:31:19,529 --> 01:31:21,464
Who was your doctor?

1621
01:31:21,464 --> 01:31:23,333
My doctor was Dr. Cho.

1622
01:31:23,333 --> 01:31:24,534
Mine was Dr. Kim.

1623
01:31:24,534 --> 01:31:25,635
(Delighted)

1624
01:31:25,635 --> 01:31:27,404
He's a renowned specialist.

1625
01:31:27,404 --> 01:31:29,039
I went to Seoul St. Mary's Hospital too.

1626
01:31:29,039 --> 01:31:30,774
Really?

1627
01:31:30,774 --> 01:31:33,209
What? You're all from the same ward?

1628
01:31:33,743 --> 01:31:35,679
- You went to St. Mary's Hospital?
- Yes.

1629
01:31:35,679 --> 01:31:36,980
(She meets a fellow hospital alum.)

1630
01:31:36,980 --> 01:31:39,616
I went to Samsung Medical Center.

1631
01:31:39,616 --> 01:31:41,451
Where were you treated, Min Young?

1632
01:31:41,451 --> 01:31:42,652
At Asan Medical Center.

1633
01:31:43,486 --> 01:31:44,788
We've covered quite a range.

1634
01:31:44,788 --> 01:31:45,622
That's right.

1635
01:31:45,622 --> 01:31:49,859
We all went to
one of the Big Five hospitals.

1636
01:31:49,859 --> 01:31:51,161
The Big Five.

1637
01:31:51,161 --> 01:31:52,228
Pretty much.

1638
01:31:52,228 --> 01:31:53,863
- The Big Five.
- Seriously.

1639
01:31:53,863 --> 01:31:54,664
(Baffled)

1640
01:31:54,664 --> 01:31:56,032
How did the conversation end up here?

1641
01:31:56,032 --> 01:31:57,033
The Big Five.

1642
01:31:57,967 --> 01:32:00,036
This is hilarious.

1643
01:32:00,036 --> 01:32:01,938
This is a conversation
only these eight could have.

1644
01:32:03,039 --> 01:32:05,075
It feels like they've grown much closer.

1645
01:32:05,975 --> 01:32:08,311
It's not like this is a college reunion.

1646
01:32:08,778 --> 01:32:11,548
I can't believe
we're even having this conversation.

1647
01:32:11,548 --> 01:32:13,149
How is this even possible?

1648
01:32:13,149 --> 01:32:15,051
So have you never dated?

1649
01:32:15,051 --> 01:32:16,619
You mean since getting sick?

1650
01:32:16,619 --> 01:32:19,089
I couldn't date while I was in treatment.

1651
01:32:19,622 --> 01:32:22,659
I couldn't meet anyone at all
during treatment.

1652
01:32:22,659 --> 01:32:24,861
- I didn't think I'd be able to date.
- Right.

1653
01:32:26,496 --> 01:32:28,164
It's not easy, is it?

1654
01:32:28,164 --> 01:32:29,232
Treatment.

1655
01:32:30,467 --> 01:32:33,103
When you're sick,
you don't have room for that.

1656
01:32:33,103 --> 01:32:34,738
I went through a breakup while I was sick.

1657
01:32:35,305 --> 01:32:36,373
(Startled)

1658
01:32:37,640 --> 01:32:40,010
Did they break up with you
after finding out you were sick?

1659
01:32:40,010 --> 01:32:41,845
I don't think the illness itself
was the reason.

1660
01:32:41,845 --> 01:32:43,847
I think it was
because of the circumstances.

1661
01:32:43,847 --> 01:32:46,049
I tried to see it
from their perspective too.

1662
01:32:46,649 --> 01:32:48,818
They were in the prime of their life,

1663
01:32:48,818 --> 01:32:50,987
at an age when there was
so much they wanted to do,

1664
01:32:50,987 --> 01:32:53,990
but staying with me meant
waiting with no end in sight.

1665
01:32:54,758 --> 01:32:58,094
It felt selfish to ask them
to take care of me.

1666
01:32:58,094 --> 01:33:00,664
And if our roles were reversed,
could I have done the same?

1667
01:33:00,664 --> 01:33:04,067
I knew how difficult it would be,
so I understood.

1668
01:33:05,969 --> 01:33:09,906
(The freedoms of youth
they had no choice but to give up)

1669
01:33:10,707 --> 01:33:12,308
I think it has its pros and cons.

1670
01:33:12,308 --> 01:33:14,477
Dating while you're sick...

1671
01:33:14,477 --> 01:33:16,179
can feel like you're putting
the other person...

1672
01:33:16,179 --> 01:33:17,981
through something terrible.

1673
01:33:17,981 --> 01:33:19,282
And there's guilt too.

1674
01:33:20,016 --> 01:33:22,252
Even if you beat the illness,

1675
01:33:22,252 --> 01:33:23,653
there are still so many hurdles.

1676
01:33:25,722 --> 01:33:26,990
It's so unfair.

1677
01:33:26,990 --> 01:33:28,058
Exactly.

1678
01:33:32,662 --> 01:33:35,365
(Breaking down the walls,)

1679
01:33:35,365 --> 01:33:40,537
(will they find love?)

1680
01:33:52,048 --> 01:33:53,216
(Surprised)

1681
01:33:53,216 --> 01:33:54,584
What?

1682
01:33:54,584 --> 01:33:55,885
What's that?

1683
01:33:58,054 --> 01:33:59,556
Another one's here.

1684
01:33:59,556 --> 01:34:00,623
We got another message.

1685
01:34:01,558 --> 01:34:05,962
(A new message has arrived at the house.)

1686
01:34:05,962 --> 01:34:07,931
Okay! Who wants to read it?

1687
01:34:09,466 --> 01:34:10,767
What's this?

1688
01:34:10,767 --> 01:34:12,268
How about Ye Ji with your lovely voice?

1689
01:34:12,268 --> 01:34:13,303
Thank you.

1690
01:34:14,571 --> 01:34:15,538
What could it be?

1691
01:34:16,139 --> 01:34:20,577
(What message arrived on the first night?)

1692
01:34:24,116 --> 01:34:27,320
Every night,
the locked Room of Time will open.

1693
01:34:28,154 --> 01:34:30,790
Only one person can enter
the Room of Time at a time.

1694
01:34:33,326 --> 01:34:34,493
The locked Room of Time?

1695
01:34:34,493 --> 01:34:37,029
Isn't that the room
that was locked before?

1696
01:34:37,330 --> 01:34:38,831
The one with that symbol on it.

1697
01:34:38,831 --> 01:34:40,867
So it's that room?

1698
01:34:41,067 --> 01:34:42,602
The locked Room of Time?

1699
01:34:44,370 --> 01:34:49,442
(The mysterious room that has yet to open)

1700
01:34:58,784 --> 01:35:02,989
(A place to be honest
about their feelings every night)

1701
01:35:02,989 --> 01:35:06,759
(Where feelings become time,)

1702
01:35:07,927 --> 01:35:12,131
(and time becomes feelings)

1703
01:35:13,900 --> 01:35:17,169
(The Room of Time)

1704
01:35:17,503 --> 01:35:19,205
(Startled)

1705
01:35:25,344 --> 01:35:28,014
- It's kind of like "Interstellar."
- It really is.

1706
01:35:33,519 --> 01:35:37,256
(The Room of Time)

1707
01:35:37,256 --> 01:35:40,426
(You have 10 minutes here.)

1708
01:35:40,426 --> 01:35:45,898
(Every day,
each man and woman receives 100 minutes.)

1709
01:35:46,365 --> 01:35:49,535
(10 ten-minute pieces of time,
given each day)

1710
01:35:54,340 --> 01:35:57,710
(To anyone you want to spend time with,)

1711
01:35:57,710 --> 01:36:02,315
(you may freely give your time.)

1712
01:36:02,782 --> 01:36:05,785
(You may give it all to one person...)

1713
01:36:05,785 --> 01:36:07,853
(or divide it among several.)

1714
01:36:07,853 --> 01:36:11,190
(Man / Woman)

1715
01:36:11,190 --> 01:36:14,160
(If the total time a man and woman
give each other reaches 100 minutes,)

1716
01:36:14,160 --> 01:36:16,229
(they will go on a date.)

1717
01:36:16,229 --> 01:36:17,730
This is so well designed.

1718
01:36:17,730 --> 01:36:19,098
This is fun.

1719
01:36:19,098 --> 01:36:21,200
You can understand it at a glance.

1720
01:36:22,435 --> 01:36:23,536
Love Against Time.

1721
01:36:23,536 --> 01:36:25,037
That gave me chills.

1722
01:36:27,006 --> 01:36:29,008
So time is how they gauge
each other's feelings.

1723
01:36:29,008 --> 01:36:30,076
Exactly.

1724
01:36:30,076 --> 01:36:32,078
Say someone gave me 60 minutes
on the first day,

1725
01:36:32,078 --> 01:36:33,346
then only ten minutes the next day.

1726
01:36:33,346 --> 01:36:34,747
I'd be like, "Wait a second."

1727
01:36:34,747 --> 01:36:35,882
"Are they losing interest in me?"

1728
01:36:35,882 --> 01:36:38,017
- You'd think that, right?
- Exactly.

1729
01:36:39,752 --> 01:36:40,753
I'd lose my mind.

1730
01:36:40,753 --> 01:36:42,221
This is driving me crazy already.

1731
01:36:42,221 --> 01:36:43,723
(Every shift in their feelings will be
laid bare in the Room of Time.)

1732
01:36:49,362 --> 01:36:52,798
(Seon Ho visits the Room of Time
on the first night.)

1733
01:36:55,801 --> 01:36:57,103
(Room of Time guide)

1734
01:36:57,103 --> 01:36:59,138
(You receive 100 minutes each day.)

1735
01:36:59,138 --> 01:37:01,407
(Use your time freely
on anyone who interests you.)

1736
01:37:01,407 --> 01:37:03,676
(Your choices here
will not be shared with anyone.)

1737
01:37:07,046 --> 01:37:08,648
You know that sense of anticipation?

1738
01:37:15,888 --> 01:37:17,924
As time went on,

1739
01:37:17,924 --> 01:37:19,792
I started to wonder
if it was the person I had in mind.

1740
01:37:19,792 --> 01:37:21,360
I couldn't shake that feeling.

1741
01:37:24,697 --> 01:37:26,265
Am I allowed to say this?

1742
01:37:27,500 --> 01:37:29,569
Actually, I know that person.

1743
01:37:30,736 --> 01:37:31,938
Who?

1744
01:37:31,938 --> 01:37:33,406
What is this?

1745
01:37:33,406 --> 01:37:34,974
"I know that person?"

1746
01:37:35,808 --> 01:37:37,043
What?

1747
01:37:37,043 --> 01:37:38,010
Wait, what is this?

1748
01:37:38,010 --> 01:37:39,545
This just got so much more interesting.

1749
01:37:40,079 --> 01:37:41,280
What?

1750
01:37:41,280 --> 01:37:42,081
What?

1751
01:37:44,050 --> 01:37:45,117
Will they remember me?

1752
01:37:50,556 --> 01:37:52,425
It brought back memories of that time,

1753
01:38:01,267 --> 01:38:02,768
and thinking it might be someone I knew,

1754
01:38:03,536 --> 01:38:05,605
I guess I felt drawn to them.

1755
01:38:08,774 --> 01:38:10,076
What are the chances?

1756
01:38:11,344 --> 01:38:12,578
This is like a movie.

1757
01:38:14,247 --> 01:38:15,548
Come on.

1758
01:38:15,548 --> 01:38:16,983
Come on.

1759
01:38:16,983 --> 01:38:19,218
Who is it?

1760
01:38:19,218 --> 01:38:20,386
What if he's mistaken?

1761
01:38:20,386 --> 01:38:22,221
- This is straight out of a movie.
- He says it's like a movie!

1762
01:38:23,589 --> 01:38:25,858
The thought I'd dismissed as impossible
turned out to be true.

1763
01:38:27,159 --> 01:38:28,327
Is this fate?

1764
01:38:33,432 --> 01:38:35,568
(Love Against Time)

1765
01:38:35,568 --> 01:38:37,003
- It's over.
- No!

1766
01:38:38,237 --> 01:38:39,839
They're ending it here?

1767
01:38:39,839 --> 01:38:41,007
That's not fair.

1768
01:38:41,007 --> 01:38:42,508
How can they end it like this?

1769
01:38:42,508 --> 01:38:44,443
I'm not leaving
until we film the final episode today.

1770
01:38:44,443 --> 01:38:45,845
(Refuses to go home)

1771
01:38:45,845 --> 01:38:47,914
But seriously...

1772
01:38:47,914 --> 01:38:48,981
Who could it be?

1773
01:38:48,981 --> 01:38:49,815
"Will they remember me?"

1774
01:38:49,815 --> 01:38:51,350
Seriously, who could it be?

1775
01:38:51,350 --> 01:38:52,084
I'm losing my mind.

1776
01:38:52,084 --> 01:38:54,020
Even a drama would get criticized
for a storyline like this.

1777
01:38:54,020 --> 01:38:55,154
It would. People would say
this could never happen.

1778
01:38:55,154 --> 01:38:56,155
A story like this.

1779
01:38:56,756 --> 01:38:57,757
This is so good.

1780
01:38:58,558 --> 01:38:59,325
This is so much fun.

1781
01:38:59,325 --> 01:39:01,794
- This is seriously so much fun.
- It is.

1782
01:39:01,794 --> 01:39:03,429
The suspense is killing me.

1783
01:39:03,429 --> 01:39:04,630
If it's really the person I know,

1784
01:39:04,630 --> 01:39:06,566
wouldn't they remember me?

1785
01:39:06,566 --> 01:39:07,733
What? You startled me.

1786
01:39:07,733 --> 01:39:08,801
(The first date finally begins.)

1787
01:39:08,801 --> 01:39:10,503
I think you're someone I know.

1788
01:39:10,503 --> 01:39:11,370
Was it me?

1789
01:39:11,370 --> 01:39:12,805
I'd even seen updates about you recently.

1790
01:39:12,805 --> 01:39:14,473
What is this? You set this up, didn't you?

1791
01:39:14,473 --> 01:39:15,575
I have goosebumps.

1792
01:39:15,575 --> 01:39:17,276
I think I really felt a spark.

1793
01:39:17,276 --> 01:39:18,744
There was just something
I was curious about.

1794
01:39:18,744 --> 01:39:20,112
(Two people who shared the same time,
Did they share the same feelings?)

1795
01:39:20,112 --> 01:39:20,980
You got the text, right?

1796
01:39:20,980 --> 01:39:22,548
I didn't think they'd actually show up.

1797
01:39:22,548 --> 01:39:23,950
What did those ten minutes mean?

1798
01:39:23,950 --> 01:39:27,153
Then they must've given 50 minutes
to someone else too.

1799
01:39:27,153 --> 01:39:29,989
To be honest, they're still in treatment.

1800
01:39:29,989 --> 01:39:32,091
Of course,
you want to be with someone healthy.

1801
01:39:32,091 --> 01:39:33,659
It's a practical concern.

1802
01:39:33,659 --> 01:39:34,694
Which makes this difficult.

1803
01:39:34,694 --> 01:39:35,795
I never thought
the thing I was worried about...

1804
01:39:35,795 --> 01:39:37,697
would come up this soon.

1805
01:39:37,697 --> 01:39:39,732
I don't know.

1806
01:39:39,732 --> 01:39:41,234
I have to talk to them, no matter what.

1807
01:39:41,234 --> 01:39:42,435
It's 100 minutes.

1808
01:39:42,768 --> 01:39:44,637
(An unexpected twist,
Who received the first 100 minutes?)

1809
01:39:44,637 --> 01:39:46,006
(Love Against Time)

1810
01:39:46,008 --> 01:39:47,008
(Subtitles are provided by KOCOWA.)
